WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you all for
your time. So, we

00:00:07.441 --> 00:00:09.390
wanted to get a conversation
going between people,

00:00:09.391 --> 00:00:12.720
and networking, optics,
hardware, distribute systems.

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.570
The main motivation here
is actually very simple.

00:00:15.571 --> 00:00:18.455
It's the fact that this
is a time of change.

00:00:18.456 --> 00:00:21.240
If you look at
almost any resource

00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:22.780
underlined cloud infrastructure,

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:25.370
whether it's compute,
networking inside

00:00:25.371 --> 00:00:27.205
the data center or
in the long hall

00:00:27.206 --> 00:00:29.355
or different kinds of storage.

00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:32.400
The dominant
technology for each of

00:00:32.401 --> 00:00:34.110
these resources is approaching

00:00:34.111 --> 00:00:36.265
the tail end of its S-curve,

00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:38.755
things up looking like a plateau.

00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:41.525
For example, if you
think about compute,

00:00:41.526 --> 00:00:43.580
the primary metric of interest

00:00:43.581 --> 00:00:45.785
is flops per dollar that
we are going to achieve.

00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:48.090
Thanks to Moore's law
and Dennard scaling,

00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:51.415
we've had a lovely ride
over the past 35 years.

00:00:51.416 --> 00:00:54.290
But because of fundamental
physical constraints

00:00:54.291 --> 00:00:56.905
on the size of transistors,

00:00:56.906 --> 00:00:59.000
the CPU dry voltage and

00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:00.200
the number of cores that we

00:01:00.201 --> 00:01:01.955
can put on individual sockets,

00:01:01.956 --> 00:01:05.035
things seem to the flat line.

00:01:05.036 --> 00:01:08.150
Now, this has actually
prompted a lot of

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.899
work in our custom accelerators,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.940
things like FPGAs and
GPUs and custom ASICs,

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:17.810
that are getting a lot of
traction in Cloud environments.

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.150
You probably heard Mark
talk about the growth

00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:22.310
of FPGAs and later
in this session,

00:01:22.311 --> 00:01:24.860
you'll hear from Tom and
Samroma about some of

00:01:24.861 --> 00:01:28.575
the FPGA innovation happening
for networking workloads.

00:01:28.576 --> 00:01:30.950
Similarly, when
you start thinking

00:01:30.951 --> 00:01:33.685
about the network
inside data centers,

00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:35.840
it relies on electrical switches.

00:01:35.841 --> 00:01:38.150
There's an analogous
phenomena that

00:01:38.151 --> 00:01:40.675
I'm terming the Moore's Law
for networking.

00:01:40.676 --> 00:01:42.975
Essentially,
every couple of years,

00:01:42.976 --> 00:01:46.400
switch vendors give us a new
switch generation that

00:01:46.401 --> 00:01:48.095
has doubled the bandwidth

00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:51.220
but the cost remains
exactly the same.

00:01:51.221 --> 00:01:54.890
Now, this is a lovely property
because it allows us

00:01:54.891 --> 00:01:57.650
to build faster and faster
networks that can

00:01:57.651 --> 00:02:00.680
keep pace with
increasing demand while

00:02:00.681 --> 00:02:04.700
keeping the overall network
cost low and constant.

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.740
That's actually one
of the assumptions

00:02:06.741 --> 00:02:08.995
underlying the Cloud
economic model.

00:02:08.996 --> 00:02:12.555
However, things could start
getting tricky very soon.

00:02:12.556 --> 00:02:15.400
To understand that, let's
look at this graph again.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.320
Historically, the increase in

00:02:18.321 --> 00:02:19.700
switch bandwidth has come from

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:21.740
the fact that we are
able to increase

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.060
the speed of the services

00:02:23.061 --> 00:02:24.645
at the edge of
the switching ASIC.

00:02:24.646 --> 00:02:26.720
The switch vendors
have always used

00:02:26.721 --> 00:02:28.965
the non-return to
zero modulation format.

00:02:28.966 --> 00:02:30.830
One is encoded as high voltage,

00:02:30.831 --> 00:02:32.665
zero as low voltage.

00:02:32.666 --> 00:02:35.360
However, the latest
generation of switches is

00:02:35.361 --> 00:02:38.140
the first generation
where we've had to

00:02:38.141 --> 00:02:40.575
result to higher order modulation

00:02:40.576 --> 00:02:42.170
because it's becoming harder and

00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:44.270
harder to increase the speed

00:02:44.271 --> 00:02:46.510
and the density of those IO pins.

00:02:46.511 --> 00:02:48.755
This is like
four level modulation,

00:02:48.756 --> 00:02:50.690
which means that
you encode two bits

00:02:50.691 --> 00:02:52.610
per symbol but it means that you

00:02:52.611 --> 00:02:54.649
have vital SNR requirements

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.060
because the levels are
getting closer and closer.

00:02:57.061 --> 00:02:59.270
This is a trick that's
hard to play over and

00:02:59.271 --> 00:03:01.770
over again because if
you go to eight levels,

00:03:01.771 --> 00:03:04.820
your SNR requirements
are even harsher.

00:03:04.821 --> 00:03:07.655
So, if you were to step back

00:03:07.656 --> 00:03:09.680
and look at network
inside data centers,

00:03:09.681 --> 00:03:11.120
the primary metric of

00:03:11.121 --> 00:03:13.925
interests is
the gigabits per dollar.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.840
We've had a lovely ride,

00:03:15.841 --> 00:03:18.380
but within the next
five or six years,

00:03:18.381 --> 00:03:21.365
so by the time we get
to 50 terabits switch

00:03:21.366 --> 00:03:23.780
generation beyond that things

00:03:23.781 --> 00:03:26.015
could start to look very dodgy.

00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:28.805
The same thing applies
to other resources.

00:03:28.806 --> 00:03:31.825
If you look at WAN capacity,

00:03:31.826 --> 00:03:33.995
we are actually approaching

00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:36.540
the Shannon capacity
of individual fibers.

00:03:36.541 --> 00:03:38.730
If you go to
the next story session,

00:03:38.731 --> 00:03:41.465
you'll hear about
fundamental physical reasons

00:03:41.466 --> 00:03:45.000
why we're approaching
capacity limits on hard drives.

00:03:45.001 --> 00:03:48.880
Now, if you're
the CTO of a company,

00:03:48.881 --> 00:03:50.255
this is a nightmare scenario.

00:03:50.256 --> 00:03:52.035
That's a perfect storm.

00:03:52.036 --> 00:03:55.185
But for researchers, this
is the perfect opportunity,

00:03:55.186 --> 00:03:57.410
because it means that

00:03:57.411 --> 00:04:01.220
if we come up with
a new technology,

00:04:01.221 --> 00:04:03.130
no matter how disruptive it is,

00:04:03.131 --> 00:04:05.405
if it leads to a new growth curve

00:04:05.406 --> 00:04:07.680
in terms of these
metrics of interest,

00:04:07.681 --> 00:04:10.270
it has a very good chance
of deployment.

00:04:10.271 --> 00:04:13.290
I think that's
a really powerful message.

00:04:13.291 --> 00:04:15.380
If that's the only message
that you take

00:04:15.381 --> 00:04:17.190
away from this talk or
even from this session,

00:04:17.191 --> 00:04:20.310
at least I and Yeebo
will be delighted.

00:04:20.311 --> 00:04:22.125
So, what I want to do

00:04:22.126 --> 00:04:24.410
next is talk to you
about a little bit about

00:04:24.411 --> 00:04:26.180
the disruptions happening in

00:04:26.181 --> 00:04:28.460
the networking space and tell you

00:04:28.461 --> 00:04:30.350
about optical innovations that

00:04:30.351 --> 00:04:31.640
we're doing inside the company,

00:04:31.641 --> 00:04:33.080
both in research and in

00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:35.340
Azure networking to
address these challenges.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.125
So, I'm going to

00:04:37.126 --> 00:04:39.840
start with the network
inside the data centers.

00:04:39.841 --> 00:04:42.080
If you remember, I told

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.060
you about this Moore's law
for networking and

00:04:44.061 --> 00:04:45.890
the fact that it has
allowed us to keep

00:04:45.891 --> 00:04:48.725
the cost of the network
low and constant.

00:04:48.726 --> 00:04:52.760
However, if electrical switches
stop scaling for free,

00:04:52.761 --> 00:04:54.495
we'll have an increase
in the cost.

00:04:54.496 --> 00:04:55.895
I'll explain the shape of

00:04:55.896 --> 00:04:58.370
this curve in a couple
of slides from now.

00:04:58.371 --> 00:05:01.615
What's really worrying
is the timing of this.

00:05:01.616 --> 00:05:03.380
Because this is expected
to happen around

00:05:03.381 --> 00:05:06.095
the same time where
the Cloud traffic

00:05:06.096 --> 00:05:07.290
not only continues to

00:05:07.291 --> 00:05:10.590
grow but it's pace
actually picks up.

00:05:10.591 --> 00:05:13.580
Because of custom
hardware FPGAs, GPUs,

00:05:13.581 --> 00:05:16.765
because if new scenarios
like large-scale learning,

00:05:16.766 --> 00:05:18.830
which means that we'll be
generating and consuming

00:05:18.831 --> 00:05:20.090
a lot more data and

00:05:20.091 --> 00:05:22.295
doing it in a power
efficient fashion.

00:05:22.296 --> 00:05:26.120
So, this is a big problem for us.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.690
One of the things or
one of the benefits

00:05:29.691 --> 00:05:32.270
of working at a place like
Microsoft research is

00:05:32.271 --> 00:05:35.120
that we always think
of mediums term

00:05:35.121 --> 00:05:38.545
strategic solutions and
long-term disruptive solutions.

00:05:38.546 --> 00:05:41.450
I wanted to give you a sense
of those examples of

00:05:41.451 --> 00:05:42.920
those solutions in the context

00:05:42.921 --> 00:05:45.160
of this problem over
the next few slides.

00:05:45.161 --> 00:05:47.240
So, to understand
a solution, let's

00:05:47.241 --> 00:05:49.270
look inside the problem
for a second.

00:05:49.271 --> 00:05:52.415
So, if you look at
inside the switch,

00:05:52.416 --> 00:05:55.370
we have a switching ASIC
that sits on

00:05:55.371 --> 00:05:57.490
a substrate or a package

00:05:57.491 --> 00:06:00.620
connected by VGA
connectors or IO pins,

00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:04.850
a PCB trace and an optical
transceiver that goes from

00:06:04.851 --> 00:06:08.870
the bits or the electronic
domains to optical domain.

00:06:08.871 --> 00:06:10.590
So, something tiny like this.

00:06:10.591 --> 00:06:13.670
Now, it is the speed
and the density of

00:06:13.671 --> 00:06:15.050
the IO pins that are causing

00:06:15.051 --> 00:06:17.215
the problem because it's
harder and harder to scale.

00:06:17.216 --> 00:06:19.490
So, the obvious thing
to do is that well,

00:06:19.491 --> 00:06:21.020
why don't we move the optics

00:06:21.021 --> 00:06:23.160
closer to the switching ASIC?

00:06:23.161 --> 00:06:25.924
Which means that you get rid
of this bottleneck resource

00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:29.285
and the switches are
easier to scale.

00:06:29.286 --> 00:06:30.965
Now, this is something
that's been discussed

00:06:30.966 --> 00:06:32.475
a lot in the optics community,

00:06:32.476 --> 00:06:34.315
on the silicon photonics space,

00:06:34.316 --> 00:06:35.840
lots of names to it,

00:06:35.841 --> 00:06:38.250
we're calling it
In-Package Optics.

00:06:38.251 --> 00:06:40.310
So essentially, what
the switch would

00:06:40.311 --> 00:06:42.575
look like two
generations from now,

00:06:42.576 --> 00:06:45.120
is that you'd have
a switching ASIC,

00:06:45.121 --> 00:06:47.025
it would be surrounded by

00:06:47.026 --> 00:06:49.985
chiplets comprising
electrical components,

00:06:49.986 --> 00:06:53.745
so all the drivers and
all the analog components.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:56.330
Then the optical
chiplets and what

00:06:56.331 --> 00:06:58.670
this would do is it
will take the data

00:06:58.671 --> 00:07:00.830
being generated by the ASIC and

00:07:00.831 --> 00:07:02.330
modulate light which is then

00:07:02.331 --> 00:07:04.730
sent 10's or 100's of meters.

00:07:04.731 --> 00:07:08.740
Now, in this case, the chiplet
is made in silicon because

00:07:08.741 --> 00:07:11.210
a few reasons to reduce costs and

00:07:11.211 --> 00:07:14.330
to make it amenable to
integration with the CMOS ASIC.

00:07:14.331 --> 00:07:16.340
What we've started
doing is actually

00:07:16.341 --> 00:07:17.915
designing our own chips.

00:07:17.916 --> 00:07:21.360
This is a silicon-based chip
designed at a lab.

00:07:21.361 --> 00:07:25.165
Is Chi here? He's
a chief designer who

00:07:25.166 --> 00:07:26.720
makes the chips for
us that are being

00:07:26.721 --> 00:07:29.645
fabricated at external foundries.

00:07:29.646 --> 00:07:31.980
Now, one of the key
technical challenges

00:07:31.981 --> 00:07:33.405
here is that, well,

00:07:33.406 --> 00:07:35.420
to do optical networking,
you need light,

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:38.255
and it's hard to generate
light in silicon.

00:07:38.256 --> 00:07:39.630
It's a fundamental problem; it's

00:07:39.631 --> 00:07:41.530
an indirect band gap material.

00:07:41.531 --> 00:07:44.540
So what we now need to
do is you need to have

00:07:44.541 --> 00:07:46.275
disaggregated transceivers

00:07:46.276 --> 00:07:48.515
because in a standard
transceiver,

00:07:48.516 --> 00:07:52.130
the laser and the modulator
is in the same package.

00:07:52.131 --> 00:07:56.405
Whereas in this case, we need
a separate light source,

00:07:56.406 --> 00:07:58.040
in this case, which happens to be

00:07:58.041 --> 00:08:00.520
shared across all the chiplets.

00:08:00.521 --> 00:08:02.470
This is cool because
it means that we

00:08:02.471 --> 00:08:04.245
can have a better light source,

00:08:04.246 --> 00:08:05.600
we can actually cool it better,

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:06.700
and I think Dan is going to tell

00:08:06.701 --> 00:08:08.010
you about more about some of

00:08:08.011 --> 00:08:11.025
the implications of
this architectural chain.

00:08:11.026 --> 00:08:12.770
Now, this is typically made in

00:08:12.771 --> 00:08:14.445
a material like indium phosphide.

00:08:14.446 --> 00:08:17.720
Again, we've started
designing our own chips,

00:08:17.721 --> 00:08:19.940
and I think this is
an animation that will show you

00:08:19.941 --> 00:08:23.500
the different layers of the chip.

00:08:23.501 --> 00:08:27.285
It comprises lasers, waveguides,
and other components.

00:08:27.286 --> 00:08:29.880
I think it's few millimeters

00:08:29.881 --> 00:08:31.200
by few millimeters high, sir?

00:08:31.201 --> 00:08:33.870
&gt;&gt; I think it's 12 by
12 millimeters.

00:08:33.871 --> 00:08:36.585
&gt;&gt; So 12 millimeters
by 12 millimeters

00:08:36.586 --> 00:08:39.345
and that's going to be shared
across the entire switch.

00:08:39.346 --> 00:08:42.750
Now, this is an example of
a medium-term solution.

00:08:42.751 --> 00:08:44.855
I want to step back
and talk about

00:08:44.856 --> 00:08:46.150
the implications of this for

00:08:46.151 --> 00:08:47.910
the overall Cloud network cost.

00:08:47.911 --> 00:08:51.289
So, historically cost
has been neutral.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:53.605
We're seeing signs
of disturbances,

00:08:53.606 --> 00:08:55.370
but if we are able to deliver on

00:08:55.371 --> 00:08:57.020
this in-package optic solution,

00:08:57.021 --> 00:08:58.495
we'd be able to extend

00:08:58.496 --> 00:08:59.540
the cost neutrality of

00:08:59.541 --> 00:09:01.935
switches for the next
five to six years.

00:09:01.936 --> 00:09:03.410
Which is great, because it

00:09:03.411 --> 00:09:05.245
means that the
world's not ending.

00:09:05.246 --> 00:09:07.220
But at some point,

00:09:07.221 --> 00:09:11.245
we'll run into the actual
physical constraints

00:09:11.246 --> 00:09:12.805
on the number of
fibers that we can

00:09:12.806 --> 00:09:15.005
align at the edge of
the switching ASIC,

00:09:15.006 --> 00:09:16.845
which means that
we'll have to have

00:09:16.846 --> 00:09:18.560
complex solutions and an increase

00:09:18.561 --> 00:09:20.325
in overall network cost.

00:09:20.326 --> 00:09:22.060
At some point, we'll run into

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:24.565
the actual CMOS scaling limit,

00:09:24.566 --> 00:09:26.260
whereby you cannot put

00:09:26.261 --> 00:09:28.880
more transistors onto
the switching ASIC,

00:09:28.881 --> 00:09:31.245
which means that we'll
have multiple ASICs on

00:09:31.246 --> 00:09:34.465
the same dye inside each pack
inside each switch,

00:09:34.466 --> 00:09:38.135
which in turn means
an increase in complexity

00:09:38.136 --> 00:09:39.990
and an increase in cost

00:09:39.991 --> 00:09:42.885
for every network
generation thereafter.

00:09:42.886 --> 00:09:44.750
That's the nightmare scenario for

00:09:44.751 --> 00:09:46.900
us, and that's where we really,

00:09:46.901 --> 00:09:49.865
really come in,
the long-term opportunity

00:09:49.866 --> 00:09:51.610
for optics and photonics.

00:09:51.611 --> 00:09:53.335
So what we really want to do is

00:09:53.336 --> 00:09:54.980
actually look at
these topologies,

00:09:54.981 --> 00:09:57.950
and instead of using
electrical packet switches,

00:09:57.951 --> 00:10:00.305
replace them with
optical circuit switches,

00:10:00.306 --> 00:10:02.355
something that's been
thought a lot about in

00:10:02.356 --> 00:10:05.275
the optics community and
in the network community.

00:10:05.276 --> 00:10:07.800
Now, one of the
advantages of moving

00:10:07.801 --> 00:10:09.890
away from CMOS
components is that you

00:10:09.891 --> 00:10:11.355
can develop
technologies that have

00:10:11.356 --> 00:10:12.920
a much higher relics and you

00:10:12.921 --> 00:10:15.345
can have a much flatter topology.

00:10:15.346 --> 00:10:17.160
This means that they can offer

00:10:17.161 --> 00:10:18.350
better performance per dollar

00:10:18.351 --> 00:10:20.090
because there are
fewer transceivers

00:10:20.091 --> 00:10:22.240
and fewer switches
in the network.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.320
Because there's no buffering
inside the network,

00:10:25.321 --> 00:10:28.670
we can offer very low and
very predictable latency,

00:10:28.671 --> 00:10:30.375
and that predictability is really

00:10:30.376 --> 00:10:32.015
important for the kinds of

00:10:32.016 --> 00:10:33.530
application scenarios that we

00:10:33.531 --> 00:10:36.230
expect to double up in
the Cloud owner experience.

00:10:36.231 --> 00:10:39.435
Finally, because we don't
have dependency on CMOS,

00:10:39.436 --> 00:10:42.740
this can actually be
a long-lasting solution.

00:10:42.741 --> 00:10:45.245
So this is actually
motivated us over

00:10:45.246 --> 00:10:48.245
the past couple of years
as a team to develop

00:10:48.246 --> 00:10:50.540
a research prototype that shows

00:10:50.541 --> 00:10:52.200
the basic feasibility of

00:10:52.201 --> 00:10:55.215
achieving off very fast
optical switching,

00:10:55.216 --> 00:10:57.560
nanoseconds switching,
so that we can match

00:10:57.561 --> 00:11:00.625
the performance of
electrical packet switches.

00:11:00.626 --> 00:11:03.210
So stuff that done a lot in

00:11:03.211 --> 00:11:04.970
the optics community but this is

00:11:04.971 --> 00:11:07.560
now taking a building
a real system artifact.

00:11:07.561 --> 00:11:11.130
Now, I do want to point out
that there's a big difference

00:11:11.131 --> 00:11:12.735
in a circuit-switched
architecture

00:11:12.736 --> 00:11:14.515
and a packet-switched
architecture.

00:11:14.516 --> 00:11:18.570
So, there's a long, long way
to go to take this from up,

00:11:18.571 --> 00:11:20.085
research for a tie
with a bunch of

00:11:20.086 --> 00:11:21.735
virus back in Cambridge to

00:11:21.736 --> 00:11:23.980
something that can connect
an actual data center

00:11:23.981 --> 00:11:25.460
and run real applications.

00:11:25.461 --> 00:11:28.625
So I just want to check
the QB expectations and check,

00:11:28.626 --> 00:11:30.530
but the IPO technology gives us

00:11:30.531 --> 00:11:34.085
the space to four to five years
needed to do that.

00:11:34.086 --> 00:11:38.700
So, I want to change tack
now quickly and move outside

00:11:38.701 --> 00:11:40.345
the data center and give you

00:11:40.346 --> 00:11:42.540
another example of this hardware,

00:11:42.541 --> 00:11:45.330
software, network
optics co-design.

00:11:45.331 --> 00:11:48.175
So, about five years ago,

00:11:48.176 --> 00:11:51.145
we used to build mega
data centers facilities,

00:11:51.146 --> 00:11:52.815
a few 100,000 servers,

00:11:52.816 --> 00:11:55.760
big land, lots of power,

00:11:55.761 --> 00:11:58.760
but increasingly we're finding
it hard to find the land,

00:11:58.761 --> 00:12:01.720
and the power, and space
for such mega facilities.

00:12:01.721 --> 00:12:03.875
So over the past couple of years,

00:12:03.876 --> 00:12:05.860
we move to the
regional architecture,

00:12:05.861 --> 00:12:08.745
where we have many
medium-sized data centers,

00:12:08.746 --> 00:12:11.900
about 100,000 servers in
the same metro region,

00:12:11.901 --> 00:12:15.190
so 80 kilometers connected
by a regional hub.

00:12:15.191 --> 00:12:18.130
Now, let's think about
the implications

00:12:18.131 --> 00:12:20.880
of this for the underlying
network architecture.

00:12:20.881 --> 00:12:23.390
So, in a traditional mega
data center we

00:12:23.391 --> 00:12:26.020
have the standard plus topology,

00:12:26.021 --> 00:12:28.455
in this case, I'm
showing four levels.

00:12:28.456 --> 00:12:30.355
In all our data centers we

00:12:30.356 --> 00:12:32.655
use optical cables
outside the rack,

00:12:32.656 --> 00:12:35.290
which means that every
switch port needs

00:12:35.291 --> 00:12:36.575
a transceiver that are shown

00:12:36.576 --> 00:12:38.860
earlier to do the
optoelectronic conversion,

00:12:38.861 --> 00:12:40.105
and this is a great transceiver.

00:12:40.106 --> 00:12:41.710
So transmits over
a single wavelength.

00:12:41.711 --> 00:12:44.770
When we do transmission
over the long haul,

00:12:44.771 --> 00:12:46.695
so when you're going
thousands of kilometers,

00:12:46.696 --> 00:12:50.250
we need a colored transceiver
which is massive

00:12:50.251 --> 00:12:53.810
power hungry and rather heavy,

00:12:53.811 --> 00:12:56.180
so I can keep this too long.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:12:58.470
On the other hand,
when you move to

00:12:58.471 --> 00:13:00.150
the regional architecture,

00:13:00.151 --> 00:13:01.775
what happens is that well, yes,

00:13:01.776 --> 00:13:04.125
you're essentially
taking this build block

00:13:04.126 --> 00:13:05.965
and doing smaller chunks,

00:13:05.966 --> 00:13:07.740
but your regional link,

00:13:07.741 --> 00:13:10.265
the one in the red, is
actually 80 kilometers.

00:13:10.266 --> 00:13:12.065
So what this means is that, yes,

00:13:12.066 --> 00:13:14.960
we can use great transceivers
inside the data centers,

00:13:14.961 --> 00:13:18.290
but now we have to use
colored power, expensive,

00:13:18.291 --> 00:13:20.170
hungry transceivers both on

00:13:20.171 --> 00:13:22.335
the van on the regional links.

00:13:22.336 --> 00:13:25.950
This is a nightmare if you
were to do this for power,

00:13:25.951 --> 00:13:28.550
and cost, and space,
and all these reasons.

00:13:28.551 --> 00:13:30.925
So, what the Azure
networking team did

00:13:30.926 --> 00:13:32.660
is they actually went out and

00:13:32.661 --> 00:13:34.390
designed their own transceivers

00:13:34.391 --> 00:13:37.150
that are customized for
the regional environment.

00:13:37.151 --> 00:13:39.085
This is a colored
transceiver that

00:13:39.086 --> 00:13:40.880
can operate over 80 kilometers.

00:13:40.881 --> 00:13:43.095
You can see it's
exactly the same form factor as

00:13:43.096 --> 00:13:46.335
the great transceiver that we
use inside the data center.

00:13:46.336 --> 00:13:50.075
Essentially, the trick
is to customize the DSP,

00:13:50.076 --> 00:13:51.525
to customize the FEC

00:13:51.526 --> 00:13:54.150
for the particular
scenario of interest.

00:13:54.151 --> 00:13:57.255
If the light's not going
tens of thousands of kilometers,

00:13:57.256 --> 00:14:00.550
why would we do chromatic
dispersion for that distance?

00:14:00.551 --> 00:14:03.050
So, those are
the kinds of designing

00:14:03.051 --> 00:14:05.380
for our scenarios that led

00:14:05.381 --> 00:14:07.540
to this innovation and
this was co-design both

00:14:07.541 --> 00:14:10.130
inside the company and
working with Inphi,

00:14:10.131 --> 00:14:11.725
who manufactures
these transceivers,

00:14:11.726 --> 00:14:12.815
and these are being
deployed in our

00:14:12.816 --> 00:14:15.085
data centers for the past year.

00:14:15.086 --> 00:14:18.075
So, the reason I chose

00:14:18.076 --> 00:14:19.335
these two examples is to give

00:14:19.336 --> 00:14:21.045
you a sense of our thinking,

00:14:21.046 --> 00:14:26.085
innovation by combining
optics with networking,

00:14:26.086 --> 00:14:30.280
distributed systems,
architecture, and hardware.

00:14:30.281 --> 00:14:32.760
We genuinely believe that to

00:14:32.761 --> 00:14:35.585
develop the next generation
of Cloud infrastructure,

00:14:35.586 --> 00:14:38.925
we will have to innovate
across the stack,

00:14:38.926 --> 00:14:42.300
and we can do this because
as a Cloud operator,

00:14:42.301 --> 00:14:44.965
we own and operate
the entire stack.

00:14:44.966 --> 00:14:48.265
As I showed you, we are
developing our own custom chips,

00:14:48.266 --> 00:14:50.210
we are building
optical components.

00:14:50.211 --> 00:14:52.095
We have designed
and are deploying

00:14:52.096 --> 00:14:54.955
our own transceivers
and our own switches,

00:14:54.956 --> 00:14:56.365
and as you'll hear,

00:14:56.366 --> 00:14:58.040
we already have our NICs.

00:14:58.041 --> 00:14:59.910
So we can deploy things on

00:14:59.911 --> 00:15:02.665
the stack on the NIC or
the sellers themselves,

00:15:02.666 --> 00:15:05.810
which allows us to
innovate across the stack.

00:15:05.811 --> 00:15:08.785
That's something that we're
doing both in our work but

00:15:08.786 --> 00:15:10.685
also in all our collaborations

00:15:10.686 --> 00:15:12.070
with academic collaborators.

00:15:12.071 --> 00:15:13.450
For example, the work
that we're doing with

00:15:13.451 --> 00:15:15.700
Dan and many of you in this room.

00:15:15.701 --> 00:15:18.740
So, that's the message
I wanted to get across.

00:15:18.741 --> 00:15:20.365
Thank you for your
time. I think I'm

00:15:20.366 --> 00:15:23.250
running out of time,
so thanks again.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.790
&gt;&gt; Do you have time
for one more question?

00:15:30.791 --> 00:15:32.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:15:32.511 --> 00:15:34.980
&gt;&gt; So, real fast.

00:15:34.981 --> 00:15:38.630
You didn't say anything
about efficient use of

00:15:38.631 --> 00:15:43.590
the optical network but even
with 100 gigabits right now,

00:15:43.591 --> 00:15:45.470
we're seeing the people
doing small rights

00:15:45.471 --> 00:15:47.050
and not many of the data lanes

00:15:47.051 --> 00:15:50.430
on this sort of wide data
path are really occupied.

00:15:50.431 --> 00:15:53.490
So you've got, say 10 data lane

00:15:53.491 --> 00:15:57.745
66 bits each and you've got
one bite of good data there,

00:15:57.746 --> 00:16:01.070
maybe 60 bytes of headers
and the rest is just empty.

00:16:01.071 --> 00:16:03.390
What can be done at that layer?

00:16:03.391 --> 00:16:05.040
&gt;&gt; That's a very good question.

00:16:05.041 --> 00:16:07.050
I'll give you two short answer
to that question,

00:16:07.051 --> 00:16:08.180
you can apply that question in

00:16:08.181 --> 00:16:10.710
the van scenario where
efficiency is very

00:16:10.711 --> 00:16:12.760
important and you really
need to think about

00:16:12.761 --> 00:16:15.030
software-defined primitives
to achieve that.

00:16:15.031 --> 00:16:18.455
I think your question was
more in the intra-DC context.

00:16:18.456 --> 00:16:20.410
One of the interesting
things about

00:16:20.411 --> 00:16:21.975
the intra-DC context is

00:16:21.976 --> 00:16:23.500
that things like
the 60-byte header

00:16:23.501 --> 00:16:25.495
or the 40-byte header come from

00:16:25.496 --> 00:16:28.805
scenarios if we are limited
to legacy protocols.

00:16:28.806 --> 00:16:30.685
If we control things

00:16:30.686 --> 00:16:33.175
like the NIC and
the underlying network,

00:16:33.176 --> 00:16:36.140
in many customized scenarios
like RDMA clusters,

00:16:36.141 --> 00:16:38.260
I would argue we should
just go to customize

00:16:38.261 --> 00:16:40.600
protocols that produce
a lot of this already.

00:16:40.601 --> 00:16:44.710
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:16:44.711 --> 00:16:47.955
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I completely agree.

00:16:47.956 --> 00:16:51.160
I think in that sense
you really need

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.530
a decoupling of the data
plane and the control plane

00:16:54.531 --> 00:16:56.765
would which allow you
to make those changes.

00:16:56.766 --> 00:16:58.405
Yes, I completely
agree on that one.

00:16:58.406 --> 00:16:59.945
Do we have one question?

00:16:59.946 --> 00:17:02.540
&gt;&gt; Maybe that's bringing
the question [inaudible].

00:17:02.541 --> 00:17:02.810
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:02.811 --> 00:17:04.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah, all right.

00:17:04.011 --> 00:17:05.940
So, let's thanks Sidhash again.

00:17:05.941 --> 00:17:07.750
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.480
&gt;&gt; The next speaker
is Tom Anderson.

00:17:12.481 --> 00:17:14.490
I believe most of you
already know him,

00:17:14.491 --> 00:17:16.810
but here's a short description.

00:17:16.811 --> 00:17:18.820
Tom Anderson's the chair in

00:17:18.821 --> 00:17:20.130
a [inaudible] and school for

00:17:20.131 --> 00:17:22.450
computer science
engineering at the UW.

00:17:22.451 --> 00:17:25.295
His research interests
span all aspects of

00:17:25.296 --> 00:17:29.055
building prod code robust and
efficient computer systems.

00:17:29.056 --> 00:17:31.060
So, he's a member of

00:17:31.061 --> 00:17:34.405
National Academy of Engineering

00:17:34.406 --> 00:17:38.260
and we know of multiple
lifetime achievement awards.

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:41.870
&gt;&gt; Great and I
think actually this

00:17:41.871 --> 00:17:45.050
will be this great counterpoint.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:49.150
I'm actually at essence

00:17:49.151 --> 00:17:50.595
an operating systems person

00:17:50.596 --> 00:17:53.290
and many of the things
that have been driving

00:17:53.291 --> 00:17:55.140
our research over time is

00:17:55.141 --> 00:17:58.420
just this recognition that
we are in the Cloud setting

00:17:58.421 --> 00:18:00.885
spending or particularly for

00:18:00.886 --> 00:18:02.250
really key applications spending

00:18:02.251 --> 00:18:04.180
huge amounts of time just

00:18:04.181 --> 00:18:05.810
doing these repetitive operations

00:18:05.811 --> 00:18:07.270
in the operating system.

00:18:07.271 --> 00:18:10.485
What's clear about
the hardware technology trends

00:18:10.486 --> 00:18:13.635
are that everything that
can be re-implemented,

00:18:13.636 --> 00:18:18.010
everything that's
of high capacity,

00:18:18.011 --> 00:18:20.570
the things that you're doing
all the time are going

00:18:20.571 --> 00:18:23.220
to be re-implemented
hardware just

00:18:23.221 --> 00:18:25.930
you can't afford to do
just general-purpose processing

00:18:25.931 --> 00:18:28.795
for things that you're
doing many, many times.

00:18:28.796 --> 00:18:30.840
You can see this with TPUs
but you're also going to see

00:18:30.841 --> 00:18:33.200
this on networking and
for operating systems.

00:18:33.201 --> 00:18:35.700
So, essentially the argument

00:18:35.701 --> 00:18:36.900
that I'm going to
try to make here,

00:18:36.901 --> 00:18:39.940
what we've been looking at
is on the protocol stack,

00:18:39.941 --> 00:18:42.840
but more broadly
I think one could

00:18:42.841 --> 00:18:43.875
ask this question more

00:18:43.876 --> 00:18:46.420
generally about
operating systems in general.

00:18:46.421 --> 00:18:49.460
I'm going to come down
from the application side

00:18:49.461 --> 00:18:51.980
rather than up from what
we can do in hardware.

00:18:51.981 --> 00:18:54.360
So, one of the things that's
really characteristic

00:18:54.361 --> 00:18:56.590
of these applications is a lot of

00:18:56.591 --> 00:18:57.630
the applications people actually

00:18:57.631 --> 00:18:58.885
run on data centers is that

00:18:58.886 --> 00:19:02.290
large amounts of a very
fine-grained communication

00:19:02.291 --> 00:19:03.890
partly just because
as you start to

00:19:03.891 --> 00:19:05.705
spread applications out over

00:19:05.706 --> 00:19:08.020
more and more nodes because
you need more and more nodes

00:19:08.021 --> 00:19:10.535
because Dennard scaling
isn't doing it for you.

00:19:10.536 --> 00:19:14.590
Now, your communication
granularity goes down

00:19:14.591 --> 00:19:20.020
and interesting dataset
that somebody in

00:19:20.021 --> 00:19:24.510
the SIGCOMM community collected
all of the RPCs inside of

00:19:24.511 --> 00:19:26.980
the Google data center
over a period of time that

00:19:26.981 --> 00:19:30.430
the median size of that
was only 300 bytes.

00:19:30.431 --> 00:19:31.830
So, generally you're doing

00:19:31.831 --> 00:19:35.950
single packet transfers
between nodes.

00:19:35.951 --> 00:19:39.320
You still want
the semantics of TCP,

00:19:39.321 --> 00:19:41.805
so that is reliable
in order delivery.

00:19:41.806 --> 00:19:44.980
It's many-to-many,
so what do you like

00:19:44.981 --> 00:19:46.570
the applications that this tries

00:19:46.571 --> 00:19:48.360
for are key-value
stores, databases,

00:19:48.361 --> 00:19:50.970
distributed analytics, and that

00:19:50.971 --> 00:19:53.510
you're pushing performance
limits on those,

00:19:53.511 --> 00:19:55.255
and then scaling up as you go.

00:19:55.256 --> 00:19:59.615
So, the question is how
you can deal with this.

00:19:59.616 --> 00:20:04.085
So, on the software
processing side,

00:20:04.086 --> 00:20:06.890
Linux, even if you ignore

00:20:06.891 --> 00:20:08.550
the VM layer because you're going

00:20:08.551 --> 00:20:11.625
to say catapult will take
care of that for you.

00:20:11.626 --> 00:20:15.385
On the VM side or
on the TCP side,

00:20:15.386 --> 00:20:17.010
you're spending
huge amounts of time doing

00:20:17.011 --> 00:20:20.220
just regular processing if you're

00:20:20.221 --> 00:20:22.720
trying to do through

00:20:22.721 --> 00:20:26.695
Linux about three and a half
microseconds per packet,

00:20:26.696 --> 00:20:28.850
and then single core performance

00:20:28.851 --> 00:20:30.470
it's not going to bail you out,

00:20:30.471 --> 00:20:31.650
so you could try to

00:20:31.651 --> 00:20:34.080
parallelize this that
has tried to run many,

00:20:34.081 --> 00:20:35.680
many connections over
many, many cores,

00:20:35.681 --> 00:20:38.555
but potentially it's
going to just end up

00:20:38.556 --> 00:20:41.980
burning lots of power and
spending a lot of effort,

00:20:41.981 --> 00:20:43.290
a lot of cost and

00:20:43.291 --> 00:20:46.065
time doing things that you
don't really need to do.

00:20:46.066 --> 00:20:49.490
So, there are lots of
proposed solutions to this.

00:20:49.491 --> 00:20:50.580
Probably, everybody in the room,

00:20:50.581 --> 00:20:53.990
but I know many of these so
one of them is kernel bypass,

00:20:53.991 --> 00:20:56.835
so DPDK or things like that.

00:20:56.836 --> 00:20:58.855
The challenge though is that

00:20:58.856 --> 00:21:00.130
what you want is something that's

00:21:00.131 --> 00:21:02.650
going to give you a
policy enforcement,

00:21:02.651 --> 00:21:04.510
that is if you're in
a multi-tenant data center

00:21:04.511 --> 00:21:05.715
you also need to worry about,

00:21:05.716 --> 00:21:08.920
well what are these
applications actually doing

00:21:08.921 --> 00:21:10.140
and can you trust them

00:21:10.141 --> 00:21:12.720
is it going to be the case
that they're going to obey,

00:21:12.721 --> 00:21:15.030
congestion control,
your behavior of

00:21:15.031 --> 00:21:17.440
your network is going to
depend on n Host behavior.

00:21:17.441 --> 00:21:18.750
So, you need something
that's going to

00:21:18.751 --> 00:21:20.555
give you policy enforcement.

00:21:20.556 --> 00:21:22.570
Some people are deploying

00:21:22.571 --> 00:21:26.100
a SmartNICs or NIC CPU
arrays and I should

00:21:26.101 --> 00:21:28.165
say actually there's
this really nice paper from

00:21:28.166 --> 00:21:31.190
MSR that Sambra's possibly
going to talk about,

00:21:31.191 --> 00:21:33.250
I'm not sure, that actually

00:21:33.251 --> 00:21:35.660
goes through essentially a
lot of the same arguments

00:21:35.661 --> 00:21:37.350
and that appeared at less than

00:21:37.351 --> 00:21:39.160
STI 18 and actually

00:21:39.161 --> 00:21:41.770
makes all of the same
arguments that we would make.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:46.890
If you try to put on your Nick
essentially CPU arrays,

00:21:46.891 --> 00:21:48.600
the challenge for
that is there is

00:21:48.601 --> 00:21:49.840
this complicated thing as

00:21:49.841 --> 00:21:51.340
you scale out
the number of CPUs or

00:21:51.341 --> 00:21:53.310
doing your TCP
processing that you

00:21:53.311 --> 00:21:55.490
have this complex
assignment problem of

00:21:55.491 --> 00:21:59.790
that to the CPU array and
limited per flow performance,

00:21:59.791 --> 00:22:02.715
that is if you potentially want

00:22:02.716 --> 00:22:04.570
really high bandwidth for

00:22:04.571 --> 00:22:06.300
a given individual flow and it's

00:22:06.301 --> 00:22:08.200
also relatively expensive
to go build those.

00:22:08.201 --> 00:22:09.900
RDMA is the one that the research

00:22:09.901 --> 00:22:11.860
community spending
most of their time on.

00:22:11.861 --> 00:22:13.660
In fact, most of
the stuff that I'm

00:22:13.661 --> 00:22:15.250
going to talk about
here is essentially

00:22:15.251 --> 00:22:16.780
trying to understand
how you would

00:22:16.781 --> 00:22:20.195
build a more flexible
RDMA engine.

00:22:20.196 --> 00:22:23.750
RDMA, the API is fine
for some things,

00:22:23.751 --> 00:22:26.230
but certainly for
these small-scale

00:22:26.231 --> 00:22:29.895
single packet RPC communication

00:22:29.896 --> 00:22:31.950
as a protocol,

00:22:31.951 --> 00:22:34.610
writing or reading from
memory isn't necessarily,

00:22:34.611 --> 00:22:36.620
but natural thing
that you would do if

00:22:36.621 --> 00:22:39.280
you wanted to build
a really fast RPC system,

00:22:39.281 --> 00:22:41.780
and then the hardware for RDMA

00:22:41.781 --> 00:22:43.940
actually bundles not so well

00:22:43.941 --> 00:22:46.740
with the congestion
control protocols

00:22:46.741 --> 00:22:48.680
that you would actually
want to use in practice,

00:22:48.681 --> 00:22:51.360
and so some people had also

00:22:51.361 --> 00:22:54.005
done TCP offload engine
to be an example,

00:22:54.006 --> 00:22:56.480
you need arguments are going to

00:22:56.481 --> 00:22:59.500
make towards a protocol agility.

00:22:59.501 --> 00:23:02.340
Ultimately, if I integrate

00:23:02.341 --> 00:23:04.800
over that whole set I
think what you want is

00:23:04.801 --> 00:23:07.805
the traditional operating
system functionality

00:23:07.806 --> 00:23:09.610
that is the feature set
of operating systems,

00:23:09.611 --> 00:23:11.730
which is multi-tenant isolation,

00:23:11.731 --> 00:23:13.035
the ability to say that

00:23:13.036 --> 00:23:15.205
the behavior of
applications doesn't affect

00:23:15.206 --> 00:23:16.960
other applications and at

00:23:16.961 --> 00:23:19.460
the same time hardware
assist that has

00:23:19.461 --> 00:23:24.360
some way of doing a flexible
reconfigurable architecture,

00:23:24.361 --> 00:23:26.100
so that you can then change

00:23:26.101 --> 00:23:27.700
your protocols as you need them,

00:23:27.701 --> 00:23:30.130
do you policy
compliance as you want,

00:23:30.131 --> 00:23:31.670
scale up connections to

00:23:31.671 --> 00:23:33.685
really large numbers
of connections,

00:23:33.686 --> 00:23:35.120
CPU efficiency in

00:23:35.121 --> 00:23:37.940
the common case and
performance efficiency,

00:23:37.941 --> 00:23:39.910
so essentially you
can think of you want

00:23:39.911 --> 00:23:42.040
the flexibility side to be
able to do whatever you

00:23:42.041 --> 00:23:44.680
want and at the other
side be able to

00:23:44.681 --> 00:23:47.430
handle the stuff at
the speeds that you want to

00:23:47.431 --> 00:23:48.950
handle them without having to

00:23:48.951 --> 00:23:51.730
do a huge amount and
I'm going to make

00:23:51.731 --> 00:23:53.990
this argument independently of

00:23:53.991 --> 00:23:56.780
the FPGA versus VLSI argument.

00:23:56.781 --> 00:23:58.950
Microsoft has done
perspective on that

00:23:58.951 --> 00:24:00.980
which is FPGAs are the feature,

00:24:00.981 --> 00:24:02.500
but in some ways

00:24:02.501 --> 00:24:03.800
the argument I'm going
to make here is going

00:24:03.801 --> 00:24:05.880
to be independent of that,

00:24:05.881 --> 00:24:08.190
so you can think of
this hardware assist

00:24:08.191 --> 00:24:09.725
at multiple levels.

00:24:09.726 --> 00:24:13.210
So, one layer and
the keynote talked about

00:24:13.211 --> 00:24:15.740
some extent which
Microsoft is already

00:24:15.741 --> 00:24:18.400
deployed widely
demonstrated this.

00:24:18.401 --> 00:24:19.480
If you're only asking about

00:24:19.481 --> 00:24:23.580
the packet processing
at the VM layer that is

00:24:23.581 --> 00:24:25.240
creating a virtual network

00:24:25.241 --> 00:24:29.515
that makes the virtual
machine look like,

00:24:29.516 --> 00:24:31.600
"Oh, okay, I've got
my own little land

00:24:31.601 --> 00:24:33.240
here that I'm going to be
able to communicate with."

00:24:33.241 --> 00:24:37.080
That set of work is
pretty regular and

00:24:37.081 --> 00:24:39.690
actually pretty demonstrable that

00:24:39.691 --> 00:24:40.955
you can move that onto hardware.

00:24:40.956 --> 00:24:42.535
What I'm going to end
up talking about is

00:24:42.536 --> 00:24:44.690
a harder problem which is, "Okay,

00:24:44.691 --> 00:24:46.640
now once you've gotten these
packets to the virtual

00:24:46.641 --> 00:24:48.590
machine," the virtual machines

00:24:48.591 --> 00:24:50.520
now spending almost
all of its time doing

00:24:50.521 --> 00:24:52.110
TCP packet processing in

00:24:52.111 --> 00:24:54.180
the virtual machine than
the guest operating system,

00:24:54.181 --> 00:24:57.200
so that's inefficient from
the customer perspective.

00:24:57.201 --> 00:25:00.020
It's like why was I burning
all the CPU time for that.

00:25:00.021 --> 00:25:02.070
So, can you take and try to

00:25:02.071 --> 00:25:07.095
do flexible hardware
acceleration inside the VM,

00:25:07.096 --> 00:25:10.655
so how you TCP packet
processing in hardware,

00:25:10.656 --> 00:25:12.690
and then you could ask

00:25:12.691 --> 00:25:13.920
a similar question about

00:25:13.921 --> 00:25:15.600
the application level processing,

00:25:15.601 --> 00:25:17.530
so you're handing this
off to an application,

00:25:17.531 --> 00:25:19.005
it's also doing

00:25:19.006 --> 00:25:21.750
essentially layer seven
packet processing,

00:25:21.751 --> 00:25:24.035
how do you want to
try to optimize that.

00:25:24.036 --> 00:25:26.520
This whole set of things
can actually be handled in

00:25:26.521 --> 00:25:28.955
this thing that I'm going
to talk called FlexNIC,

00:25:28.956 --> 00:25:30.120
but it's just a model for how

00:25:30.121 --> 00:25:31.360
you would do
the programming for it.

00:25:31.361 --> 00:25:33.000
Then, I'm also going to talk

00:25:33.001 --> 00:25:34.820
a little bit about how you
could do this flexibility at

00:25:34.821 --> 00:25:37.590
network switches and
in general where

00:25:37.591 --> 00:25:41.290
this is going to tie in
with the optical side is

00:25:41.291 --> 00:25:42.970
where you do have congestion

00:25:42.971 --> 00:25:44.430
that is where you
have large amounts of

00:25:44.431 --> 00:25:46.400
communication coming
in generally at

00:25:46.401 --> 00:25:49.155
the top of rack
switch or whatever.

00:25:49.156 --> 00:25:50.820
You are going to need
to be able to do

00:25:50.821 --> 00:25:53.185
something on the level
of trying to do

00:25:53.186 --> 00:25:55.540
resource allocation
in some fashion

00:25:55.541 --> 00:25:58.290
largely in the
programmable space.

00:25:58.291 --> 00:26:00.840
Okay, the overarching lesson
here is going to

00:26:00.841 --> 00:26:03.195
be the common case packet
handling is systolic,

00:26:03.196 --> 00:26:05.705
which is to say that
individual packet

00:26:05.706 --> 00:26:08.010
that the steps that
you're taking to do

00:26:08.011 --> 00:26:10.755
packet processing
is regularizable

00:26:10.756 --> 00:26:14.025
and that can be pipelined
not just packet to packet,

00:26:14.026 --> 00:26:15.910
but even within a packet
that you can do

00:26:15.911 --> 00:26:18.975
regular communication and
as a consequence of that,

00:26:18.976 --> 00:26:21.590
that you will end
up being able to do

00:26:21.591 --> 00:26:25.865
large amounts of
high bandwidth operation.

00:26:25.866 --> 00:26:28.460
Even on a single flow
because you're

00:26:28.461 --> 00:26:32.175
pipelining individual
stages of computation.

00:26:32.176 --> 00:26:34.150
I'll come back to
this point that I

00:26:34.151 --> 00:26:35.995
made earlier, which is that,

00:26:35.996 --> 00:26:38.530
anything that can be regularized,

00:26:38.531 --> 00:26:40.080
and you're spending
a lot of time on,

00:26:40.081 --> 00:26:41.930
will eventually be
built into hardware.

00:26:41.931 --> 00:26:43.380
It's just how the economics

00:26:43.381 --> 00:26:45.135
of data center's going to work.

00:26:45.136 --> 00:26:49.010
So now, the question is,
how you can go about this?

00:26:49.011 --> 00:26:51.740
This will happen both
on NICs and switches.

00:26:51.741 --> 00:26:53.750
Okay. So, the programming
model we started

00:26:53.751 --> 00:26:56.400
with is, it's usually
reconfigurable.

00:26:56.401 --> 00:26:57.470
Multi-stage pipelines are not

00:26:57.471 --> 00:26:59.465
the only people
working in the space.

00:26:59.466 --> 00:27:01.800
You take this packet
stream coming in,

00:27:01.801 --> 00:27:04.580
you have a programmable parser

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:08.740
that goes through some number
of stages of computation.

00:27:08.741 --> 00:27:13.005
These computational stages
are protocol agnostic.

00:27:13.006 --> 00:27:16.085
You can run essentially
about a terabit per

00:27:16.086 --> 00:27:17.615
second in a single pipeline,

00:27:17.616 --> 00:27:19.010
Barefoot's demonstrated that.

00:27:19.011 --> 00:27:20.560
So, from a NIC perspective,

00:27:20.561 --> 00:27:22.640
that is from an in-house
perspective, that's pretty good.

00:27:22.641 --> 00:27:24.980
That'll carry you pretty far.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.025
The idea is going to have
predictable performance.

00:27:28.026 --> 00:27:30.165
The stages all
executed in parallel.

00:27:30.166 --> 00:27:31.720
You can think of essentially,

00:27:31.721 --> 00:27:33.750
each successive packet,

00:27:33.751 --> 00:27:36.110
it goes through the stage
in the sequence.

00:27:36.111 --> 00:27:39.410
As a consequence, from
a single flow perspective,

00:27:39.411 --> 00:27:41.895
you're still processing
everything in order,

00:27:41.896 --> 00:27:43.605
as opposed to
spreading it out over

00:27:43.606 --> 00:27:46.240
multiple pipelines
or multiple CPUs,

00:27:46.241 --> 00:27:49.420
which you might be doing
in a smartNIC operation.

00:27:49.421 --> 00:27:50.620
Then, you're having to make

00:27:50.621 --> 00:27:52.030
sure that all of the packets for

00:27:52.031 --> 00:27:54.970
given flow go through
the same sequence,

00:27:54.971 --> 00:27:56.484
or on the same processor,

00:27:56.485 --> 00:27:57.790
just for this kind of

00:27:57.791 --> 00:28:00.805
state management
ordering constraints.

00:28:00.806 --> 00:28:04.695
Okay. So, what are
these match action systems do?

00:28:04.696 --> 00:28:07.410
You can think of them
the kind of the simple cases,

00:28:07.411 --> 00:28:08.620
that they can steer packets,

00:28:08.621 --> 00:28:10.179
they can initiate DMA,

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.630
trigger replies, or whatever.

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:16.085
This is an application
that's trying

00:28:16.086 --> 00:28:19.300
to write on a particular port,

00:28:19.301 --> 00:28:20.750
then what I should
do with this is to

00:28:20.751 --> 00:28:22.230
hash it to a particular core,

00:28:22.231 --> 00:28:25.925
and then DMA to a cue
or something like that.

00:28:25.926 --> 00:28:28.480
Because they're regular, again,

00:28:28.481 --> 00:28:30.365
in a systolic model,

00:28:30.366 --> 00:28:31.750
you don't want loops.

00:28:31.751 --> 00:28:33.270
There's no complicated
arithmetic.

00:28:33.271 --> 00:28:34.160
You have to be careful about

00:28:34.161 --> 00:28:35.295
what you're doing with state.

00:28:35.296 --> 00:28:37.130
You can have an arbitrary
number of stages.

00:28:37.131 --> 00:28:38.400
It's not programming the way

00:28:38.401 --> 00:28:39.675
we would normally think of it,

00:28:39.676 --> 00:28:42.700
rather you're programming
for hardware.

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:50.315
So, we designed a part of
this from a NIC perspective,

00:28:50.316 --> 00:28:52.425
how you would actually
make this work.

00:28:52.426 --> 00:28:55.160
For the NIC actually has
multiple pipelines coming in.

00:28:55.161 --> 00:28:59.455
So, it has data coming
in for received data,

00:28:59.456 --> 00:29:01.000
that is, you're having
to do some set of

00:29:01.001 --> 00:29:02.315
pipeline stages for packets

00:29:02.316 --> 00:29:03.475
that arriving from the network.

00:29:03.476 --> 00:29:05.210
Similarly, the host itself is

00:29:05.211 --> 00:29:08.455
also delivering
packets into the NIC.

00:29:08.456 --> 00:29:11.190
So, we have some pipeline
for handling that.

00:29:11.191 --> 00:29:14.120
There's a transmit pipeline
that actually send data out.

00:29:14.121 --> 00:29:16.410
There's also a pipeline
to try to do,

00:29:16.411 --> 00:29:19.410
essentially programmable
NIC behavior.

00:29:19.411 --> 00:29:21.740
You can imagine if
you were to try to

00:29:21.741 --> 00:29:25.250
decompose what happens
on an RDMA packet,

00:29:25.251 --> 00:29:27.880
if that was your protocol

00:29:27.881 --> 00:29:29.815
that you were trying
to implement?

00:29:29.816 --> 00:29:33.605
What happens on an RDMA packet
is essentially demux,

00:29:33.606 --> 00:29:35.565
you're parsing the packet,

00:29:35.566 --> 00:29:37.800
and then demuxing
on an element of

00:29:37.801 --> 00:29:39.480
the packet that
tell the DMA engine

00:29:39.481 --> 00:29:41.405
to do a particular operation.

00:29:41.406 --> 00:29:43.640
That step is something that's

00:29:43.641 --> 00:29:46.095
pretty similar to what

00:29:46.096 --> 00:29:48.095
a TCP packet does when
you arrive, like,

00:29:48.096 --> 00:29:49.560
"Okay, I'm going to
get this packet in,

00:29:49.561 --> 00:29:51.365
and I want to decide which

00:29:51.366 --> 00:29:54.850
Q this is going to go into
based on the port number."

00:29:54.851 --> 00:29:56.880
Then, based on that, and

00:29:56.881 --> 00:30:00.445
maybe the sequence
number in the packet,

00:30:00.446 --> 00:30:02.970
and then that's going to tell
me where to go from there.

00:30:02.971 --> 00:30:05.300
So, what this then
allows me to do is,

00:30:05.301 --> 00:30:06.790
to do this transform
these packets

00:30:06.791 --> 00:30:08.035
efficiently in software

00:30:08.036 --> 00:30:11.065
DMA directly in and out of
application data structure.

00:30:11.066 --> 00:30:12.725
So, it looks like it

00:30:12.726 --> 00:30:15.555
has all the same benefits
that RDMA has,

00:30:15.556 --> 00:30:18.545
and then send acknowledgement
out on the NIC.

00:30:18.546 --> 00:30:21.940
But potentially, you can
implement TCP with it.

00:30:21.941 --> 00:30:24.080
So, if you look at
what TCP is doing,

00:30:24.081 --> 00:30:26.140
it's got a lot going on.

00:30:26.141 --> 00:30:28.865
You're opening,
closing connections,

00:30:28.866 --> 00:30:30.585
you've got locking on socket API,

00:30:30.586 --> 00:30:32.825
you've got IP routing
or firewalls like

00:30:32.826 --> 00:30:35.790
congestion control,
very complicated connections.

00:30:35.791 --> 00:30:37.730
They often in the kernel,

00:30:37.731 --> 00:30:38.935
if you look at the kernel code.

00:30:38.936 --> 00:30:43.580
You're doing multiple steps,

00:30:43.581 --> 00:30:45.295
you're chasing data structures,

00:30:45.296 --> 00:30:47.200
you're doing pointer
chasing a lot.

00:30:47.201 --> 00:30:48.780
It's like, "Oh, okay,
how's this going to end

00:30:48.781 --> 00:30:50.980
up working in this kind
of systolic model?"

00:30:50.981 --> 00:30:52.690
The model we've taken is one

00:30:52.691 --> 00:30:54.530
that we've taken
essentially split out

00:30:54.531 --> 00:30:56.550
the operating system code into

00:30:56.551 --> 00:30:59.115
a fast path that's regularizable,

00:30:59.116 --> 00:31:00.565
and then a slow
path for everything

00:31:00.566 --> 00:31:02.225
that's not regularizable.

00:31:02.226 --> 00:31:04.200
Then, hopefully you
could say, "Oh, okay,

00:31:04.201 --> 00:31:06.590
the slow path just
handles offline."

00:31:06.591 --> 00:31:07.750
So, you can think
essentially of the

00:31:07.751 --> 00:31:08.970
fast path doing all of

00:31:08.971 --> 00:31:12.565
the normal operations you
would do on every data packet,

00:31:12.566 --> 00:31:14.115
generating data segments,

00:31:14.116 --> 00:31:15.865
applying rate limits,
and so forth.

00:31:15.866 --> 00:31:18.520
Collecting congestion
statistics, all of these,

00:31:18.521 --> 00:31:21.000
ending up running
on the NIC itself.

00:31:21.001 --> 00:31:23.350
Then, the slow path
running in the kernel

00:31:23.351 --> 00:31:25.585
that handles essentially all of

00:31:25.586 --> 00:31:30.090
the other connection or
control plane operations.

00:31:30.091 --> 00:31:31.510
Then, the application ends up

00:31:31.511 --> 00:31:34.075
doing some additional
amount of work.

00:31:34.076 --> 00:31:35.780
One of the aspects of

00:31:35.781 --> 00:31:37.090
this is to say that
you're splitting up

00:31:37.091 --> 00:31:41.155
congestion control in a way
that splits the work,

00:31:41.156 --> 00:31:43.295
so that instead of
thinking that you're

00:31:43.296 --> 00:31:45.705
doing congestion
control every packet,

00:31:45.706 --> 00:31:50.530
you can think that you're doing
it on a round-trip basis.

00:31:50.531 --> 00:31:53.190
So, collecting information
on the data path,

00:31:53.191 --> 00:31:55.750
and then using it on
a background basis

00:31:55.751 --> 00:31:57.260
to enforce congestion control.

00:31:57.261 --> 00:31:58.710
But, that's actually
sufficient for

00:31:58.711 --> 00:32:01.630
implementing the protocols
that we have.

00:32:01.631 --> 00:32:04.090
So, this is the kind
of performance graph,

00:32:04.091 --> 00:32:06.250
all end up leaving here,

00:32:06.251 --> 00:32:10.820
which is essentially
how well this can do.

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:13.500
This is Linux here,

00:32:13.501 --> 00:32:16.130
scaling up to 16 core
is not very well.

00:32:16.131 --> 00:32:19.450
Then, a software implementation

00:32:19.451 --> 00:32:20.850
of what I've just described,

00:32:20.851 --> 00:32:22.840
which actually does enforce

00:32:22.841 --> 00:32:26.635
congestion control compared
to software version

00:32:26.636 --> 00:32:31.430
of just the DDPA TCP version

00:32:31.431 --> 00:32:33.640
that doesn't enforce
congestion control.

00:32:33.641 --> 00:32:35.295
They actually do reasonably well,

00:32:35.296 --> 00:32:38.990
and then it's a hardware version
is that line over there.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:46.460
All of this is actually just
emulated rather than other.

00:32:46.461 --> 00:32:48.450
You can actually think
about a bunch of

00:32:48.451 --> 00:32:50.990
these issues also happening
inside of the network itself,

00:32:50.991 --> 00:32:54.430
so switches themselves
can also be programmable.

00:32:54.431 --> 00:32:56.085
You can ask this question of

00:32:56.086 --> 00:32:58.365
what can we do inside
of the network

00:32:58.366 --> 00:33:03.465
using a reprogrammable hardware
to do different things?

00:33:03.466 --> 00:33:05.490
One of the things that
we've been looking at is to

00:33:05.491 --> 00:33:08.795
do essentially
approximate fair queuing.

00:33:08.796 --> 00:33:10.210
So, fair queuing is
one of these things,

00:33:10.211 --> 00:33:12.785
the kind of longstanding,

00:33:12.786 --> 00:33:14.420
maybe 30, 40 years ago,

00:33:14.421 --> 00:33:16.765
I'm not actually sure
exactly when it was.

00:33:16.766 --> 00:33:17.520
&gt;&gt; Thirty.

00:33:17.521 --> 00:33:18.820
&gt;&gt; Thirty? Thirty
years ago, okay.

00:33:18.821 --> 00:33:21.125
So, I dated from

00:33:21.126 --> 00:33:23.450
almost before I graduated
from grad school.

00:33:23.451 --> 00:33:26.650
So, that's how long ago it was.

00:33:26.651 --> 00:33:28.500
But, the idea is that,

00:33:28.501 --> 00:33:29.960
you get isolation that is

00:33:29.961 --> 00:33:32.010
more predictable behavior out

00:33:32.011 --> 00:33:33.770
of your network if
you're able to say

00:33:33.771 --> 00:33:36.585
that if you're queuing
discipline and how you

00:33:36.586 --> 00:33:38.050
handle packets inside
of the network

00:33:38.051 --> 00:33:39.850
at a base for queuing.

00:33:39.851 --> 00:33:41.565
The challenge for it is that,

00:33:41.566 --> 00:33:43.305
it's really difficult to
implement these things

00:33:43.306 --> 00:33:46.795
in any kind of fashion.

00:33:46.796 --> 00:33:48.550
This is just an animation of what

00:33:48.551 --> 00:33:50.990
happens as you go
about doing this.

00:33:50.991 --> 00:33:53.580
In order to get
fair queuing to work,

00:33:53.581 --> 00:33:56.465
I'll just go forward from this.

00:33:56.466 --> 00:33:57.940
The different pieces, you need to

00:33:57.941 --> 00:33:59.470
have a sorted packet buffer,

00:33:59.471 --> 00:34:01.965
you need to store an update
per flow counters,

00:34:01.966 --> 00:34:04.635
you need to track global state

00:34:04.636 --> 00:34:06.135
that updates on every packet,

00:34:06.136 --> 00:34:07.635
and all of that ends up like,

00:34:07.636 --> 00:34:08.815
"Okay, how are you
going to end up

00:34:08.816 --> 00:34:11.630
implementing that
multi-gigabit speeds,

00:34:11.631 --> 00:34:12.715
or you're talking about
trying to implement

00:34:12.716 --> 00:34:14.815
it at many gigabits?"

00:34:14.816 --> 00:34:18.180
So, the argument is that,

00:34:18.181 --> 00:34:19.840
you could potentially
use this kind of

00:34:19.841 --> 00:34:22.305
reconfigurable match
action model to

00:34:22.306 --> 00:34:24.815
simulate fair queuing or
approximate fair queuing.

00:34:24.816 --> 00:34:27.450
I'm not going to actually
do the example here,

00:34:27.451 --> 00:34:30.085
but I'll just skip by it.

00:34:30.086 --> 00:34:31.190
But the idea is, basically,

00:34:31.191 --> 00:34:32.680
to use multiple queues to try

00:34:32.681 --> 00:34:34.585
to approximate the behavior,

00:34:34.586 --> 00:34:36.060
store approximate per flow

00:34:36.061 --> 00:34:38.675
counters and tables,
and so forth.

00:34:38.676 --> 00:34:40.355
So, there's a lot of research

00:34:40.356 --> 00:34:41.675
that people are doing in terms of

00:34:41.676 --> 00:34:42.820
trying to understand
what you can do

00:34:42.821 --> 00:34:45.675
with re-programmable switches.

00:34:45.676 --> 00:34:48.310
The outcome of this
ends up being,

00:34:48.311 --> 00:34:49.730
so this table here,

00:34:49.731 --> 00:34:52.400
this is actually
flows size on the x-axis,

00:34:52.401 --> 00:34:55.815
and then normalized completion
time on the y-axis,

00:34:55.816 --> 00:34:57.545
so smaller is better.

00:34:57.546 --> 00:34:59.980
Then, AFQ, which
is the blue line.

00:34:59.981 --> 00:35:03.130
So, as you get bigger flows,

00:35:03.131 --> 00:35:05.515
then, of course, it takes
longer to complete.

00:35:05.516 --> 00:35:09.620
But, one of the things
that you end up seeing is,

00:35:09.621 --> 00:35:11.670
even for things like DCTCP,

00:35:11.671 --> 00:35:13.220
which is kind of the best

00:35:13.221 --> 00:35:16.560
known congestion control
protocol for data centers,

00:35:16.561 --> 00:35:18.260
you can do better
as a consequence of

00:35:18.261 --> 00:35:21.005
having more intelligence
happening inside of the switch.

00:35:21.006 --> 00:35:22.910
Okay, and I'll just
leave it there.

00:35:22.911 --> 00:35:35.765
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
Any questions? Cool.

00:35:35.766 --> 00:35:37.305
Though I have one.

00:35:37.306 --> 00:35:38.505
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:35:38.506 --> 00:35:42.030
&gt;&gt; So you talked about Flex TCP.

00:35:42.031 --> 00:35:45.690
So maybe I missed it.

00:35:45.691 --> 00:35:49.150
Ideally, totally
kernel bypass technique or is

00:35:49.151 --> 00:35:51.310
it to as they're rating

00:35:51.311 --> 00:35:55.930
their kernel stack
using the hardware?

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.650
&gt;&gt; The enforcement,
and you can think of

00:35:58.651 --> 00:36:00.305
as the equivalent of virtue.

00:36:00.306 --> 00:36:03.390
I think, probably,
the best analogy

00:36:03.391 --> 00:36:06.945
for this is there was a period
about 40 or 50 years ago,

00:36:06.946 --> 00:36:10.120
where we decided that
what we needed to do

00:36:10.121 --> 00:36:13.200
for multiprogramming was to
have virtual memory hardware.

00:36:13.201 --> 00:36:15.070
There was a period
where people didn't

00:36:15.071 --> 00:36:17.140
know what that virtual
memory hardware look like.

00:36:17.141 --> 00:36:19.225
Like what exactly were
the obstructions?

00:36:19.226 --> 00:36:21.410
What we ended up with was

00:36:21.411 --> 00:36:23.640
this notion of having essentially

00:36:23.641 --> 00:36:27.330
table-driven enforcement
mechanism that's

00:36:27.331 --> 00:36:29.560
controlled by the
operating system kernel.

00:36:29.561 --> 00:36:31.960
That model is essentially

00:36:31.961 --> 00:36:33.445
the one that we're
moving towards,

00:36:33.446 --> 00:36:35.080
which is we're trying
to come up with

00:36:35.081 --> 00:36:37.320
some new set of constraints,

00:36:37.321 --> 00:36:39.165
some new model for how

00:36:39.166 --> 00:36:41.955
operating systems are going
to interact with networks.

00:36:41.956 --> 00:36:43.310
That going to be

00:36:43.311 --> 00:36:45.320
these control plans
operating in the kernel,

00:36:45.321 --> 00:36:48.160
that's the enforcement
mechanism that says,

00:36:48.161 --> 00:36:50.050
"Here's how much bandwidth

00:36:50.051 --> 00:36:51.925
you're going to get
on a particular flow.

00:36:51.926 --> 00:36:54.350
These are the logic of what

00:36:54.351 --> 00:36:56.710
you're able to do from
an application perspective."

00:36:56.711 --> 00:37:00.150
Then, everything else,

00:37:00.151 --> 00:37:01.780
what the application
actually does itself

00:37:01.781 --> 00:37:03.550
is all done at full speed.

00:37:03.551 --> 00:37:06.210
So what VM hardware does
essentially is to say,

00:37:06.211 --> 00:37:07.915
"Look, in the normal case,

00:37:07.916 --> 00:37:09.500
you're executing at
the full speed of

00:37:09.501 --> 00:37:11.710
the hardware even
though I'm providing

00:37:11.711 --> 00:37:15.585
this protection layer with
some hardware assist."

00:37:15.586 --> 00:37:16.670
The question that we're

00:37:16.671 --> 00:37:18.065
ending up trying
to figure out is,

00:37:18.066 --> 00:37:20.140
what is that hardware
assist that you need to

00:37:20.141 --> 00:37:22.610
be able to implement
this at full speed?

00:37:22.611 --> 00:37:25.980
&gt;&gt; Okay. All, right. Yeah.

00:37:25.981 --> 00:37:29.910
&gt;&gt; Okay. Over there,
we have someone.

00:37:29.911 --> 00:37:31.070
&gt;&gt; Oh, sorry. Kahn, did you want?

00:37:31.071 --> 00:37:32.595
&gt;&gt; Let me pass the microphone.

00:37:32.596 --> 00:37:34.350
&gt;&gt; I have the microphone.

00:37:34.351 --> 00:37:35.005
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:35.006 --> 00:37:36.350
&gt;&gt; So, I'll ask
my question anyway.

00:37:36.351 --> 00:37:39.390
So, a lot of dialogue
about RDMA centers

00:37:39.391 --> 00:37:43.735
on NIC use of resources that
are really living in the OS,

00:37:43.736 --> 00:37:46.340
such as the page table entries,

00:37:46.341 --> 00:37:50.150
and actually the
specific DMA mappings,

00:37:50.151 --> 00:37:53.130
and then the DMA
permissions records

00:37:53.131 --> 00:37:55.240
which are all tables
within the stricts to

00:37:55.241 --> 00:37:57.940
exceed the amount of SRAM
available and most of

00:37:57.941 --> 00:37:59.280
these NICs don't have time to go

00:37:59.281 --> 00:38:01.295
off to DRAM on an average packet.

00:38:01.296 --> 00:38:02.780
Do you have an opinion on all

00:38:02.781 --> 00:38:04.135
of that, where is it trending?

00:38:04.136 --> 00:38:06.580
&gt;&gt; Yes. Some person,

00:38:06.581 --> 00:38:08.805
so if you want to
start coming up.

00:38:08.806 --> 00:38:10.960
There was a really nice, again,

00:38:10.961 --> 00:38:14.770
this NSDI paper of looking
at how they map catapult.

00:38:14.771 --> 00:38:18.840
They have a two level
connection state model

00:38:18.841 --> 00:38:21.430
where the SRAM is for
the active connections

00:38:21.431 --> 00:38:23.930
and then they have
a DRAM backup for other ones.

00:38:23.931 --> 00:38:25.620
I think, once you
start to think that

00:38:25.621 --> 00:38:26.930
your operating system is managing

00:38:26.931 --> 00:38:28.180
hundreds of thousands of packets,

00:38:28.181 --> 00:38:29.340
you're needing to do some kind

00:38:29.341 --> 00:38:32.795
of a two-stage model for it.

00:38:32.796 --> 00:38:34.670
So, I think, that also is

00:38:34.671 --> 00:38:36.640
a really interesting
question of, well,

00:38:36.641 --> 00:38:39.460
if everybody is actively using

00:38:39.461 --> 00:38:41.770
tens of thousands or
hundreds of thousands of

00:38:41.771 --> 00:38:44.135
packet of connections
simultaneously,

00:38:44.136 --> 00:38:46.430
then does this kind of
caching model work,

00:38:46.431 --> 00:38:48.340
and how do you make that work?

00:38:48.341 --> 00:38:51.750
Now, that upside is that
even if you have to

00:38:51.751 --> 00:38:56.070
fetch your your connection state

00:38:56.071 --> 00:38:58.495
in from DRAM to do some work,

00:38:58.496 --> 00:39:01.820
as long as it's
relatively compact,

00:39:01.821 --> 00:39:05.990
the thing that you might be
doing over time would be

00:39:05.991 --> 00:39:11.050
some regular amount of

00:39:11.051 --> 00:39:12.360
operation that the amount of time

00:39:12.361 --> 00:39:13.410
that a connection is actually

00:39:13.411 --> 00:39:16.090
actively using the NIC is
going to be relatively small.

00:39:16.091 --> 00:39:18.970
So, the only number of
active connections you might be

00:39:18.971 --> 00:39:20.330
doing inside of the NIC at

00:39:20.331 --> 00:39:22.245
any point in time
might not be large.

00:39:22.246 --> 00:39:24.135
There are also
large amounts of like

00:39:24.136 --> 00:39:27.440
just regular protocol
issues that come

00:39:27.441 --> 00:39:28.875
from trying to run

00:39:28.876 --> 00:39:30.855
large numbers of
connections simultaneously,

00:39:30.856 --> 00:39:32.605
like TCB is just basically broken

00:39:32.606 --> 00:39:35.110
for once you get above
a thousand connections.

00:39:35.111 --> 00:39:38.260
We ran a really simple
tests that just

00:39:38.261 --> 00:39:39.880
doing simple RPCs between

00:39:39.881 --> 00:39:41.420
two nodes with
a thousand connections,

00:39:41.421 --> 00:39:44.115
and Linux will literally
starve connections,

00:39:44.116 --> 00:39:48.030
like, "Okay, is that
the best we can do?

00:39:48.031 --> 00:39:51.645
Hopefully not." Okay. Great.

00:39:51.646 --> 00:39:53.610
&gt;&gt; It's time for a last question.

00:39:53.611 --> 00:39:54.900
&gt;&gt; Oh, our last question.

00:39:54.901 --> 00:39:56.460
&gt;&gt; This is just a quick
question. What you're proposing

00:39:56.461 --> 00:39:58.735
in FlexNICs [inaudible] P4,

00:39:58.736 --> 00:40:01.720
are you trying to use
the same programming abstraction

00:40:01.721 --> 00:40:03.980
of P4 at the host site?

00:40:03.981 --> 00:40:06.405
&gt;&gt; So, you can think of
it as very similar to P4.

00:40:06.406 --> 00:40:08.660
I think the difference
is P4 doesn't

00:40:08.661 --> 00:40:11.670
really have a model for
how to program DMA.

00:40:11.671 --> 00:40:14.100
So, we added essentially it's

00:40:14.101 --> 00:40:16.780
P4 plus the kind of
DMA programming.

00:40:16.781 --> 00:40:21.410
&gt;&gt; Okay. Let's thank Tom again.

