00:40:33.251 --> 00:40:36.135
working in the area of
Azure IT networking.

00:40:36.136 --> 00:40:38.780
So, in Microsoft, she
has primary work on

00:40:38.781 --> 00:40:41.610
network offloading
technologies especially

00:40:41.611 --> 00:40:43.880
in the area of
network virtualization,

00:40:43.881 --> 00:40:45.840
such as virtual machine cues,

00:40:45.841 --> 00:40:51.030
single route SIoT and
generic pro table offloading.

00:40:51.031 --> 00:40:54.960
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Hi, like he
said, my name is Sambrahma.

00:40:54.961 --> 00:40:57.275
I'm a tech lead in
Azure networking.

00:40:57.276 --> 00:41:00.000
I specifically work in
the Host Net Working Group.

00:41:00.001 --> 00:41:01.735
For the last couple of years,

00:41:01.736 --> 00:41:03.580
what we've been working on is to

00:41:03.581 --> 00:41:05.980
offload the software
stack that we

00:41:05.981 --> 00:41:10.779
have in the Azure hosts
to an FPG SmartNIC,

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:14.480
and deploying this entirely
in our Azure data centers,

00:41:14.481 --> 00:41:17.410
and seeing great benefits
out of it in performance and

00:41:17.411 --> 00:41:22.110
latency through put per
packets per second, etc.

00:41:22.111 --> 00:41:24.035
So, I'll go into
some of the details

00:41:24.036 --> 00:41:26.545
about how we went about
implementing this.

00:41:26.546 --> 00:41:28.280
So, the agenda for my talk will

00:41:28.281 --> 00:41:30.415
be a little bit about
the Azure background.

00:41:30.416 --> 00:41:33.420
How the problems that
we have in scale,

00:41:33.421 --> 00:41:35.380
and the SDN stack,

00:41:35.381 --> 00:41:38.485
the weights implemented
today in the software stack,

00:41:38.486 --> 00:41:40.900
and what are some of the
challenges we face around

00:41:40.901 --> 00:41:43.640
that in packet processing

00:41:43.641 --> 00:41:46.000
at a per packet level
in the host.

00:41:46.001 --> 00:41:47.540
We'll also talk about

00:41:47.541 --> 00:41:50.025
what other considerations
we employ to

00:41:50.026 --> 00:41:51.890
determine that FPG SmartNIC is

00:41:51.891 --> 00:41:54.405
the right choice for
us to go forward,

00:41:54.406 --> 00:41:56.090
and then I'll go into
some of the details of

00:41:56.091 --> 00:41:59.220
what the architecture and
the design of the SmartNIC.

00:41:59.221 --> 00:42:01.110
Then, we have a lot
of lessons learned

00:42:01.111 --> 00:42:03.090
because this is pretty
much the first time

00:42:03.091 --> 00:42:04.170
where we are deploying at

00:42:04.171 --> 00:42:08.490
such a scale software and
hardware project coexistence,

00:42:08.491 --> 00:42:10.940
and deploying hardware at

00:42:10.941 --> 00:42:12.950
such a scale to be
able to debug it,

00:42:12.951 --> 00:42:15.130
monitor it, have alerts,

00:42:15.131 --> 00:42:18.525
and add an agile wave
which is like

00:42:18.526 --> 00:42:23.000
our cornerstone of
Azure and networking.

00:42:23.001 --> 00:42:25.070
So, this is the
generic slide that we

00:42:25.071 --> 00:42:26.740
use for the Azure services.

00:42:26.741 --> 00:42:29.120
So, we have at the bottom
most layer the infrastructure

00:42:29.121 --> 00:42:31.979
service which
includes our storage,

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:34.090
compute, networking, et cetera.

00:42:34.091 --> 00:42:37.535
On top of it, we have
the data services and

00:42:37.536 --> 00:42:39.380
providing the app services

00:42:39.381 --> 00:42:43.120
like Azure ML,
analytics, et cetera.

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:47.540
So, Azure and networking has
come a long way since it

00:42:47.541 --> 00:42:50.550
was first deployed
in terms of compute.

00:42:50.551 --> 00:42:53.730
We have started from
like 10K or 100K of

00:42:53.731 --> 00:42:57.645
VMs and now we're at millions
of virtual machines.

00:42:57.646 --> 00:42:59.890
Azure storage has
seen the same rate of

00:42:59.891 --> 00:43:01.890
growth as well as
the networking where

00:43:01.891 --> 00:43:06.570
we're seeing from terabytes
of data to petabytes today.

00:43:06.571 --> 00:43:09.200
So, this is a generic
marketing slide

00:43:09.201 --> 00:43:11.080
that we use and we see

00:43:11.081 --> 00:43:12.630
at what rate the Azure is

00:43:12.631 --> 00:43:15.240
growing on a day-to-day
basis literally.

00:43:15.241 --> 00:43:16.995
We have to have support for

00:43:16.996 --> 00:43:19.070
all the Linux VMs because most of

00:43:19.071 --> 00:43:23.265
the virtual machines that
are out there are Linux VMs.

00:43:23.266 --> 00:43:28.030
We see a great growth in
terms of the deployment,

00:43:28.031 --> 00:43:29.955
onboarding of new customers,

00:43:29.956 --> 00:43:33.710
and the different kinds of
features and requirements that

00:43:33.711 --> 00:43:35.570
we have in terms of Azure

00:43:35.571 --> 00:43:37.930
and networking
specifically as well.

00:43:37.931 --> 00:43:41.775
So, that would show us like
the agility that we may

00:43:41.776 --> 00:43:43.360
need to maintain because lot of

00:43:43.361 --> 00:43:45.755
the features that we add
are customer-driven,

00:43:45.756 --> 00:43:47.840
either through issues
that they face

00:43:47.841 --> 00:43:49.960
or the new features
that they need

00:43:49.961 --> 00:43:52.410
to move from their current model

00:43:52.411 --> 00:43:55.085
on-prem network to
the Azure network.

00:43:55.086 --> 00:43:59.390
So, other ways to
think about scale.

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:02.575
It's not just about deploying

00:44:02.576 --> 00:44:05.445
a service or deploying a feature,

00:44:05.446 --> 00:44:08.940
it's about how do
we handle failures,

00:44:08.941 --> 00:44:10.540
how do we detect, monitor,

00:44:10.541 --> 00:44:13.880
and is a continuous feedback
loop that we need to be in.

00:44:13.881 --> 00:44:17.635
Believe me, we often get
our on-call rotations,

00:44:17.636 --> 00:44:19.245
and it's not pretty
to live through

00:44:19.246 --> 00:44:21.200
a one week of
an on-call rotation,

00:44:21.201 --> 00:44:25.380
because if you don't
have a backup of knowing

00:44:25.381 --> 00:44:28.405
how to automate this detection

00:44:28.406 --> 00:44:30.485
of heart failures like switch,

00:44:30.486 --> 00:44:33.210
link, node failures, the

00:44:33.211 --> 00:44:34.960
crashing of any
software components

00:44:34.961 --> 00:44:36.495
in the nodes, et cetera,

00:44:36.496 --> 00:44:38.475
at such a large scale,

00:44:38.476 --> 00:44:41.935
it's literally impossible
to do manually or

00:44:41.936 --> 00:44:43.010
even not having

00:44:43.011 --> 00:44:46.330
a super sophisticated
automated solution for that.

00:44:46.331 --> 00:44:52.065
So, another customer goal
would be to actually,

00:44:52.066 --> 00:44:55.720
one is detection of these
failures and mitigation of it.

00:44:55.721 --> 00:44:58.320
So, most likely,
we would prefer to

00:44:58.321 --> 00:45:01.020
know about an issue before
a customer even notices it.

00:45:01.021 --> 00:45:02.640
So, we need to have
the automation in

00:45:02.641 --> 00:45:05.745
place to detect these conditions
that are going on,

00:45:05.746 --> 00:45:08.440
some heart failures, and
be able to mitigate it,

00:45:08.441 --> 00:45:10.660
which would be like a service
heal the virtual machine to

00:45:10.661 --> 00:45:13.180
a different node or in our case,

00:45:13.181 --> 00:45:14.970
for example, switch
back from let's say,

00:45:14.971 --> 00:45:16.180
if the hardware is misbehaving,

00:45:16.181 --> 00:45:18.550
switch back to the software
path et cetera.

00:45:18.551 --> 00:45:20.535
Then, we always have the problem

00:45:20.536 --> 00:45:22.810
of soft failures which
are not obvious.

00:45:22.811 --> 00:45:26.085
Obviously, if the link is
down between two switches,

00:45:26.086 --> 00:45:27.980
it's easy to detect, easy to fix.

00:45:27.981 --> 00:45:29.680
But if there are
soft failures that

00:45:29.681 --> 00:45:31.600
we see very often is like

00:45:31.601 --> 00:45:33.640
one in 100 packets is getting

00:45:33.641 --> 00:45:36.315
corrupted by one extra byte
or something like that.

00:45:36.316 --> 00:45:38.760
Though sometimes I've
seen even in practice,

00:45:38.761 --> 00:45:41.530
those go unnoticed
until, for example,

00:45:41.531 --> 00:45:44.245
in our case when we offloaded
this flow to an FPGA,

00:45:44.246 --> 00:45:46.090
the FPGA was adding an extra

00:45:46.091 --> 00:45:47.725
32 bytes at the end
of the packet.

00:45:47.726 --> 00:45:49.550
That went unnoticed until

00:45:49.551 --> 00:45:51.710
some TLS type of
checksum was happening,

00:45:51.711 --> 00:45:53.500
and then at
the customer endpoint,

00:45:53.501 --> 00:45:55.265
they would see an effect of this.

00:45:55.266 --> 00:45:57.680
So, how do we detect
this kind of failures?

00:45:57.681 --> 00:45:59.980
A lot of issues that
we see are in latency.

00:45:59.981 --> 00:46:02.010
So, generally, everything
is working fine.

00:46:02.011 --> 00:46:04.520
Occasionally, the latency
peaks at 50 milliseconds

00:46:04.521 --> 00:46:07.680
or something like that in
a TCP type of operation.

00:46:07.681 --> 00:46:09.340
So how do we detect that?

00:46:09.341 --> 00:46:11.300
How do we monitor
that at that scale?

00:46:11.301 --> 00:46:13.290
That's also very important.

00:46:13.291 --> 00:46:14.900
So, I think again,

00:46:14.901 --> 00:46:17.460
I'm stressing the automation
is the key for all this.

00:46:17.461 --> 00:46:19.440
The other thing is
the IaaS customers,

00:46:19.441 --> 00:46:21.425
they are highly intolerant to

00:46:21.426 --> 00:46:24.490
any virtual machine downtime
or any networking downtime.

00:46:24.491 --> 00:46:26.850
So, whenever we have
to service any of

00:46:26.851 --> 00:46:28.390
our components and we have a lot

00:46:28.391 --> 00:46:30.135
of components
including in the host,

00:46:30.136 --> 00:46:32.575
in the washing machine,
in the host,

00:46:32.576 --> 00:46:34.420
and now in the
hardware et cetera,

00:46:34.421 --> 00:46:36.305
the serviceability is the key.

00:46:36.306 --> 00:46:37.920
We cannot afford to bring down

00:46:37.921 --> 00:46:39.590
the host, we cannot reboot,

00:46:39.591 --> 00:46:41.220
so we try to update

00:46:41.221 --> 00:46:43.650
all our components
reboot lessly except for

00:46:43.651 --> 00:46:45.970
the Os which also we tried to do

00:46:45.971 --> 00:46:48.730
it in such a fashion that the
downtime is extremely less.

00:46:48.731 --> 00:46:51.505
Any networking components being

00:46:51.506 --> 00:46:55.330
updated or being serviced
need to guarantee that

00:46:55.331 --> 00:46:58.960
we do not see a network outage
to the virtual machine

00:46:58.961 --> 00:47:01.260
and we try our best to actually

00:47:01.261 --> 00:47:03.750
keep the TCP connections alive
so that's the goal of it.

00:47:03.751 --> 00:47:05.010
Basically, the VM should not

00:47:05.011 --> 00:47:08.645
really be able to see
a TCP connection drop.

00:47:08.646 --> 00:47:12.984
So, the key to this
is performance.

00:47:12.985 --> 00:47:16.080
Everything is measured
at four to five nines

00:47:16.081 --> 00:47:18.900
and on a customer basis which
includes the performance,

00:47:18.901 --> 00:47:21.025
the network performance,
the host performance,

00:47:21.026 --> 00:47:24.390
the VM availability
whether they should

00:47:24.391 --> 00:47:27.420
not suffer any downtime

00:47:27.421 --> 00:47:31.140
and the serviceability
of all our components.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:35.915
So, how do we achieve
SDN in Azure?

00:47:35.916 --> 00:47:39.225
So, today, what we do is what
we call is the host SDN.

00:47:39.226 --> 00:47:41.630
So, our data Plane
runs in the host,

00:47:41.631 --> 00:47:43.430
we have distributed controllers

00:47:43.431 --> 00:47:44.790
that are running
across the region.

00:47:44.791 --> 00:47:49.665
So, for example, the SLB
has original SLB manager.

00:47:49.666 --> 00:47:51.170
For any vignette programming,

00:47:51.171 --> 00:47:53.290
there is a regional
network manager,

00:47:53.291 --> 00:47:55.180
et cetera that is per region.

00:47:55.181 --> 00:47:58.215
We have controllers running
at each of the hosts.

00:47:58.216 --> 00:48:02.070
So, any of the configuration
that comes along from

00:48:02.071 --> 00:48:04.530
the management plane which
is using the rest API

00:48:04.531 --> 00:48:07.390
like a portal or CLI,

00:48:07.391 --> 00:48:10.105
however, the deployment occurs
for the virtual machine,

00:48:10.106 --> 00:48:13.280
that comes to the regional
network manager eventually and

00:48:13.281 --> 00:48:17.900
those policies are programmed
to upper host level.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:21.430
So, the controller on
the host programs,

00:48:21.431 --> 00:48:24.560
our virtual switch that
runs in the host and

00:48:24.561 --> 00:48:25.940
the data plane in

00:48:25.941 --> 00:48:28.550
the virtual switch is which
applies the policies.

00:48:28.551 --> 00:48:31.010
So, obviously, then you have

00:48:31.011 --> 00:48:34.490
this data plane needs to
apply the flow policy to

00:48:34.491 --> 00:48:36.055
millions of VMs and that's

00:48:36.056 --> 00:48:38.095
only achievable because
we are handling it

00:48:38.096 --> 00:48:39.600
at the host level for

00:48:39.601 --> 00:48:43.060
whatever number of VMs that
are running on the host.

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:47.335
So, this is a diagram
of our software stack,

00:48:47.336 --> 00:48:49.890
this runs in each of the host.

00:48:49.891 --> 00:48:51.765
So, we have the
virtual machines hosted

00:48:51.766 --> 00:48:55.260
on the Azure node or
the host as we call it.

00:48:55.261 --> 00:48:57.690
So, before a hardware
acceleration,

00:48:57.691 --> 00:48:59.490
what is presented to
the virtual machine

00:48:59.491 --> 00:49:01.510
is a synthetic NIC
or a software NIC.

00:49:01.511 --> 00:49:03.980
Any packet that needs
to come in and out of

00:49:03.981 --> 00:49:05.300
the VM is always going

00:49:05.301 --> 00:49:07.015
through the software
stack in the VM,

00:49:07.016 --> 00:49:08.720
that goes to the hypervisor,

00:49:08.721 --> 00:49:10.480
to the host and in the host

00:49:10.481 --> 00:49:12.385
we have what we call
as a VMSwitch which is

00:49:12.386 --> 00:49:15.000
a layer two switch
which basically

00:49:15.001 --> 00:49:18.745
is really a delivery mechanism
in and out of the VM.

00:49:18.746 --> 00:49:20.900
All the policies that I told that

00:49:20.901 --> 00:49:22.715
get applied to the controllers,

00:49:22.716 --> 00:49:26.345
the controller programs
using a VFP API which is

00:49:26.346 --> 00:49:31.110
a simple match action table API.

00:49:31.111 --> 00:49:33.770
So, the controllers program

00:49:33.771 --> 00:49:35.620
the VFP with that information.

00:49:35.621 --> 00:49:37.820
So, like I said, there
could be different kinds

00:49:37.821 --> 00:49:40.090
of controllers like something
for load balancing,

00:49:40.091 --> 00:49:42.490
something for ravine it customer,

00:49:42.491 --> 00:49:45.010
address to the provider,
address mapping,

00:49:45.011 --> 00:49:47.100
ACLs, metering et cetera.

00:49:47.101 --> 00:49:48.560
So, each of the controllers can

00:49:48.561 --> 00:49:50.680
program their own rules and

00:49:50.681 --> 00:49:52.160
those go into different layers of

00:49:52.161 --> 00:49:54.405
VFP and they're
independent of each other.

00:49:54.406 --> 00:49:56.390
So, if you were to
add a new feature,

00:49:56.391 --> 00:49:58.740
we could have
a new layer and none of

00:49:58.741 --> 00:49:59.900
the other controllers
would have to

00:49:59.901 --> 00:50:01.825
change any of their policy.

00:50:01.826 --> 00:50:04.150
So, in VFP, what would

00:50:04.151 --> 00:50:06.300
happen is so let's take
an example of a packet

00:50:06.301 --> 00:50:09.955
being transmitted from the VM
that would land from the VM

00:50:09.956 --> 00:50:11.090
over what we call as

00:50:11.091 --> 00:50:14.540
a virtual machine bus to
the host from the hypervisor.

00:50:14.541 --> 00:50:16.915
In the host when it
hits the VMSwitch,

00:50:16.916 --> 00:50:20.230
VFP is built as
an extension to VMSwitch.

00:50:20.231 --> 00:50:22.670
So, originally when
we started Azure,

00:50:22.671 --> 00:50:24.240
all of the policy enforcement

00:50:24.241 --> 00:50:26.340
was done as part of VMSwitch.

00:50:26.341 --> 00:50:29.065
Then, due to different
constraints because

00:50:29.066 --> 00:50:33.240
the VMSwitch update requires
a reboot et cetera,

00:50:33.241 --> 00:50:35.000
so we went with
this model of having

00:50:35.001 --> 00:50:38.420
a pluggable virtual filtering
platform within VMSwitch.

00:50:38.421 --> 00:50:40.450
So, every packet
that VMSwitch sees,

00:50:40.451 --> 00:50:43.895
in inbound and outbound
path is routed through VFP.

00:50:43.896 --> 00:50:46.000
VFP is the one that is the brains

00:50:46.001 --> 00:50:48.280
of the host SDN on the host.

00:50:48.281 --> 00:50:49.840
So, it applies all the policies,

00:50:49.841 --> 00:50:50.810
it goes through the layers.

00:50:50.811 --> 00:50:52.570
For example, on an
outbound packet,

00:50:52.571 --> 00:50:54.120
it would go through
the ACL layer,

00:50:54.121 --> 00:50:56.535
modify the packet based
on the configuration,

00:50:56.536 --> 00:50:58.160
go to the VNET layer, modify

00:50:58.161 --> 00:51:01.165
that packet let's say
map the CA to the PA.

00:51:01.166 --> 00:51:03.390
This might also need
some communication to

00:51:03.391 --> 00:51:06.340
the regional control plane.

00:51:06.341 --> 00:51:08.120
For example, you might not have

00:51:08.121 --> 00:51:10.000
the mapping of this
destination VM,

00:51:10.001 --> 00:51:11.665
you might need to wait for

00:51:11.666 --> 00:51:14.480
that configuration to
come back and then apply

00:51:14.481 --> 00:51:16.780
that change to that packet
and then go to

00:51:16.781 --> 00:51:17.840
the SLB layer so

00:51:17.841 --> 00:51:20.315
any VIP to DIP mapping
would occur there.

00:51:20.316 --> 00:51:23.650
So, this processing allows us to

00:51:23.651 --> 00:51:26.350
do multiple layers independent

00:51:26.351 --> 00:51:28.400
of different kinds of
controllers programming it.

00:51:28.401 --> 00:51:30.760
Then we go one step further than

00:51:30.761 --> 00:51:33.155
this that once this packet
goes through it,

00:51:33.156 --> 00:51:35.370
if you see a connection
setup packet like

00:51:35.371 --> 00:51:37.785
in the case of TCP
being the Syn packet,

00:51:37.786 --> 00:51:40.230
you know that this is
a packet and these are

00:51:40.231 --> 00:51:42.230
the flow ID for

00:51:42.231 --> 00:51:44.390
this packet and the end result

00:51:44.391 --> 00:51:45.810
is always going to be the same.

00:51:45.811 --> 00:51:49.280
So, ACL, VNET, SLB
the NAT layer combine

00:51:49.281 --> 00:51:51.235
it and what is the final action

00:51:51.236 --> 00:51:52.825
that is called as
a transposition,

00:51:52.826 --> 00:51:54.100
we can save that information

00:51:54.101 --> 00:51:55.250
so that you don't have to go to

00:51:55.251 --> 00:51:58.595
the individual layers after
the connection setup is done.

00:51:58.596 --> 00:52:00.550
So, we call this
technology call as

00:52:00.551 --> 00:52:02.040
a Unified Flow which is

00:52:02.041 --> 00:52:03.520
actually the basis for

00:52:03.521 --> 00:52:06.400
offloading to
the accelerated links.

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:12.060
So, this again explains
how we would go through

00:52:12.061 --> 00:52:14.540
the layering occur
both in the inbound

00:52:14.541 --> 00:52:17.470
and outbound so the VFP
is also the forwarder.

00:52:17.471 --> 00:52:19.320
So once, it does
the outbound processing

00:52:19.321 --> 00:52:21.150
for a packet, it
forwards a packet,

00:52:21.151 --> 00:52:23.100
it determines a target
and then does

00:52:23.101 --> 00:52:24.425
the inbound processing based

00:52:24.426 --> 00:52:26.230
on the rules set
up for that port.

00:52:26.231 --> 00:52:28.180
So, the target could either

00:52:28.181 --> 00:52:30.420
be another VM on
the same host for example,

00:52:30.421 --> 00:52:33.945
if they were do VM to VM and
we're seeing more of this in

00:52:33.946 --> 00:52:38.010
particular requirements or
it could be another VM on

00:52:38.011 --> 00:52:39.880
a VNET in Azure within

00:52:39.881 --> 00:52:41.280
the region or another region or

00:52:41.281 --> 00:52:42.835
it could be some
internet endpoint.

00:52:42.836 --> 00:52:45.650
So, it doesn't matter.
Basically, the VFP is just

00:52:45.651 --> 00:52:47.590
doing a purport
policy application

00:52:47.591 --> 00:52:49.575
on inbound and outbound.

00:52:49.576 --> 00:52:54.895
So, this just shows you
the flow of a packet.

00:52:54.896 --> 00:52:57.360
So, for example, the ACLS
would be applied,

00:52:57.361 --> 00:53:00.010
the different action is
specified for that layer,

00:53:00.011 --> 00:53:02.220
for example, drops
certain packets

00:53:02.221 --> 00:53:04.750
with 10.416 address et cetera.

00:53:04.751 --> 00:53:07.940
Then the action at
the load balancer layer

00:53:07.941 --> 00:53:11.420
and then the VNET mapping
would occur at the end layer.

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:17.580
So, what do we see as
challenges in this?

00:53:17.581 --> 00:53:19.820
This works fine at 1G, at 10G,

00:53:19.821 --> 00:53:22.270
it works fine, but what
do we see as challenges?

00:53:22.271 --> 00:53:26.550
As the networking
speed increases,

00:53:26.551 --> 00:53:28.740
there are more and more packets
being generated,

00:53:28.741 --> 00:53:31.625
but there's not enough CPU
to handle this load.

00:53:31.626 --> 00:53:33.350
What are the options for us?

00:53:33.351 --> 00:53:35.575
So, we would prefer to
offload this to hardware,

00:53:35.576 --> 00:53:38.360
but when we offload it
to hardware using ASICs,

00:53:38.361 --> 00:53:40.370
you are having a one or two years

00:53:40.371 --> 00:53:43.040
of cycle to update the ASICs.

00:53:43.041 --> 00:53:44.505
It might not be sufficient,

00:53:44.506 --> 00:53:47.170
what are the features available
in the ASICs, et cetera.

00:53:47.171 --> 00:53:52.885
So, we decided to look at
FPGAs to solve this problem.

00:53:52.886 --> 00:53:54.270
So, what we have is today,

00:53:54.271 --> 00:53:57.575
we'd still have the ASIC NIC

00:53:57.576 --> 00:54:01.965
that provides the access
to the virtual machine.

00:54:01.966 --> 00:54:03.610
There is a technology
called Single-Root

00:54:03.611 --> 00:54:05.350
IO Virtualization which allows

00:54:05.351 --> 00:54:06.865
the virtual functions or PC

00:54:06.866 --> 00:54:08.990
virtual function to
be exposed to the VM.

00:54:08.991 --> 00:54:11.130
So, the packets from the VM can

00:54:11.131 --> 00:54:13.385
directly access the
network to and from it.

00:54:13.386 --> 00:54:14.630
But if it did that,

00:54:14.631 --> 00:54:16.240
then it will be
bypassing the host and

00:54:16.241 --> 00:54:18.125
there would be nobody
applying the policies.

00:54:18.126 --> 00:54:20.305
So, we go with this bump
in the wire model

00:54:20.306 --> 00:54:23.010
that the delivery of the
packets and reception of

00:54:23.011 --> 00:54:25.540
the packets from
the VM still occurs

00:54:25.541 --> 00:54:28.745
on the virtual function
and the existing NIC.

00:54:28.746 --> 00:54:31.710
Any of the offloads that the NIC
can provide for example,

00:54:31.711 --> 00:54:34.115
receives site scaling
like CPU steering,

00:54:34.116 --> 00:54:36.825
task offloads like
checksum, et cetera,

00:54:36.826 --> 00:54:39.580
or lots of offload, that can
still occur at that NIC,

00:54:39.581 --> 00:54:42.615
but the policy application
on a per packet basis

00:54:42.616 --> 00:54:46.370
occurs on an FPGA that's
programmed by RVFP.

00:54:46.371 --> 00:54:49.375
We use this API
called the GFP API

00:54:49.376 --> 00:54:52.300
which converts the unified flow
that we talked about in

00:54:52.301 --> 00:54:57.240
the VFP to the settings

00:54:57.241 --> 00:54:59.405
basically what
the FPG understands.

00:54:59.406 --> 00:55:01.620
These are some of
the measurements

00:55:01.621 --> 00:55:02.770
that we got from running

00:55:02.771 --> 00:55:05.485
regular open-source tools like

00:55:05.486 --> 00:55:06.885
iPerf and so forth

00:55:06.886 --> 00:55:08.820
and you see I'll just
go quickly over this.

00:55:08.821 --> 00:55:11.050
Like you see the compute D series

00:55:11.051 --> 00:55:13.180
gets up to 25G and we see

00:55:13.181 --> 00:55:15.550
improvements in
the latency especially

00:55:15.551 --> 00:55:18.455
because we have SR-IOV totally
bypass the whole stack.

00:55:18.456 --> 00:55:22.295
So, we see per packet
per second improvement,

00:55:22.296 --> 00:55:23.760
and then you
obviously also reduce

00:55:23.761 --> 00:55:25.135
jitter because you don't have

00:55:25.136 --> 00:55:27.450
any software issues of getting

00:55:27.451 --> 00:55:30.385
descheduled by hypervisor to
run VM processing et cetera.

00:55:30.386 --> 00:55:32.950
So, this gives us more cores
for the VM processing

00:55:32.951 --> 00:55:36.345
offloads all the network
to the hardware.

00:55:36.346 --> 00:55:39.925
Then, since the VM can get
near native performance,

00:55:39.926 --> 00:55:41.005
we can also include

00:55:41.006 --> 00:55:45.200
other workloads that needed
that kind of performance.

00:55:45.201 --> 00:55:47.710
So, this is an example of
what, on the left side,

00:55:47.711 --> 00:55:50.420
you have how the packets
transmission and

00:55:50.421 --> 00:55:51.580
reception looks like in

00:55:51.581 --> 00:55:53.740
the case where it's being
processed by the software.

00:55:53.741 --> 00:55:55.080
So, anything coming out of

00:55:55.081 --> 00:55:57.150
the VM goes to
the virtual switch,

00:55:57.151 --> 00:55:59.860
does the processing, then goes
out of the physical layer.

00:55:59.861 --> 00:56:01.765
But in the case of
accelerated networking,

00:56:01.766 --> 00:56:03.430
it just goes to
the network card and

00:56:03.431 --> 00:56:06.345
directly out of
the TOR and beyond.

00:56:06.346 --> 00:56:08.610
So, this is an example of how

00:56:08.611 --> 00:56:10.435
the GFT actually
does the processing.

00:56:10.436 --> 00:56:13.080
So, like I said, the controllers
program VFP as of today,

00:56:13.081 --> 00:56:14.305
there's no change over there.

00:56:14.306 --> 00:56:17.045
VFP still needs to
be there in the path

00:56:17.046 --> 00:56:18.325
of what we call as

00:56:18.326 --> 00:56:21.210
Exception Packets which is
the connection setup packets,

00:56:21.211 --> 00:56:24.300
and for TCP it is
SYN, SYN-ACK packets.

00:56:24.301 --> 00:56:26.300
For UDP, it's the first
packet that we see.

00:56:26.301 --> 00:56:28.950
So, we are right now
offloading just TCP and UDP,

00:56:28.951 --> 00:56:31.600
and potentially we would
do RDMA flows as well.

00:56:31.601 --> 00:56:33.150
So, like I said,

00:56:33.151 --> 00:56:35.110
the unified flow gets created in

00:56:35.111 --> 00:56:38.240
the VFP when it does
receive the first packet,

00:56:38.241 --> 00:56:39.480
so it goes through the slope out

00:56:39.481 --> 00:56:41.390
processing in across each layers,

00:56:41.391 --> 00:56:43.295
generates this unified
flow which is,

00:56:43.296 --> 00:56:45.510
this is a flow and
this is the action,

00:56:45.511 --> 00:56:48.240
and that is what it
configures to the GFT.

00:56:48.241 --> 00:56:52.825
So, GFT is the engine running
inside the FPGA SmartNIC.

00:56:52.826 --> 00:56:55.590
So, when FPGA receives
this packet from the VM,

00:56:55.591 --> 00:56:56.970
let's say an outbound packet,

00:56:56.971 --> 00:56:58.400
it goes through its table,

00:56:58.401 --> 00:57:01.260
there's a multi-layer
hash table lookup.

00:57:01.261 --> 00:57:03.535
So, like we were seeing
on the SRAM and then,

00:57:03.536 --> 00:57:05.950
we have an L1 cache and
you don't find the packet,

00:57:05.951 --> 00:57:08.065
go to the DRAM lookup that,

00:57:08.066 --> 00:57:09.740
and if you don't find that packet

00:57:09.741 --> 00:57:11.755
or a flow for
that particular packet,

00:57:11.756 --> 00:57:14.195
that's tagged as
an exception packet and

00:57:14.196 --> 00:57:17.785
sent to the whole stack
for VFP to process.

00:57:17.786 --> 00:57:19.570
If it does find a packet which is

00:57:19.571 --> 00:57:21.595
when VFP offloads this flow,

00:57:21.596 --> 00:57:23.770
then it does the
transformation itself,

00:57:23.771 --> 00:57:26.745
bypasses the stack, and
sends it out on the TOR.

00:57:26.746 --> 00:57:28.210
Similarly, if you
receive a packet,

00:57:28.211 --> 00:57:29.555
the same mechanism happens,

00:57:29.556 --> 00:57:32.900
but when it will indicate
the packet to the ASIC NIC

00:57:32.901 --> 00:57:34.720
which is using the SR-IOV

00:57:34.721 --> 00:57:36.485
and directly indicating
the packet to VM,

00:57:36.486 --> 00:57:38.870
again we bypass the host.

00:57:39.450 --> 00:57:43.070
Off the key thing for
this is serviceability.

00:57:43.071 --> 00:57:44.930
So, serviceability is a key is

00:57:44.931 --> 00:57:47.460
because we need to be
able to update any of

00:57:47.461 --> 00:57:52.800
the components without any kind
of downtime for the VM,

00:57:52.801 --> 00:57:55.350
because we have to- so,

00:57:55.351 --> 00:57:57.020
I have just one minute,
so I'll quickly go over

00:57:57.021 --> 00:57:59.075
some of the lessons we
learned in this area as well.

00:57:59.076 --> 00:58:00.660
So, one of that is basically,

00:58:00.661 --> 00:58:02.900
if the VM cannot suffer downtime,

00:58:02.901 --> 00:58:04.350
then we need to have a failover,

00:58:04.351 --> 00:58:06.350
simple failover from
the hardware to

00:58:06.351 --> 00:58:07.850
the software part when any of

00:58:07.851 --> 00:58:10.495
the key components
are being serviced.

00:58:10.496 --> 00:58:13.720
So, that was one of
the key things that we did

00:58:13.721 --> 00:58:16.230
as part of the initial design
because we also

00:58:16.231 --> 00:58:18.250
need a service because
the whole idea of using

00:58:18.251 --> 00:58:21.080
FPGA is to be innovate
as fast as we can

00:58:21.081 --> 00:58:23.915
which means our goal
is to service

00:58:23.916 --> 00:58:25.460
the entire stack every month

00:58:25.461 --> 00:58:27.200
in the spring fashion, right?

00:58:27.201 --> 00:58:30.550
So, some of the lessons that
we learned is I would say,

00:58:30.551 --> 00:58:32.975
the most important is
monitoring is really the key.

00:58:32.976 --> 00:58:34.320
It's not in
my bullet points here,

00:58:34.321 --> 00:58:37.460
but monitoring is the key
because at this scale,

00:58:37.461 --> 00:58:39.600
when you see in
generally, in hardware,

00:58:39.601 --> 00:58:40.740
we tend to think that okay,

00:58:40.741 --> 00:58:42.310
you need to get a repro.

00:58:42.311 --> 00:58:43.660
All that's not going
to happen, right?

00:58:43.661 --> 00:58:45.195
Like customers facing this issue,

00:58:45.196 --> 00:58:46.930
you've got to figure it
out so we need to have

00:58:46.931 --> 00:58:49.400
as much as monitoring alerts

00:58:49.401 --> 00:58:51.105
and notifications
that we can have.

00:58:51.106 --> 00:58:53.445
The failure rate has
remained quite low,

00:58:53.446 --> 00:58:56.715
and we have some alerts for
that the RAM failing over

00:58:56.716 --> 00:58:58.590
some time or some of

00:58:58.591 --> 00:59:00.020
the parts that like

00:59:00.021 --> 00:59:01.440
oscillators and stuff
like that failing.

00:59:01.441 --> 00:59:03.180
So, we're kind of
monitoring that like what

00:59:03.181 --> 00:59:05.600
the thing is for that.

00:59:05.601 --> 00:59:07.500
I think, another thing
is like it's

00:59:07.501 --> 00:59:10.340
a mindset change as
well because to work in

00:59:10.341 --> 00:59:12.090
the co-exist in fashion between

00:59:12.091 --> 00:59:13.630
hardware engineers and
software engineers

00:59:13.631 --> 00:59:15.085
to build a team around that,

00:59:15.086 --> 00:59:17.450
that had like initial challenges

00:59:17.451 --> 00:59:19.535
to understand each
other, et cetera.

00:59:19.536 --> 00:59:23.000
That is an iterative problem
that you resolve,

00:59:23.001 --> 00:59:25.230
and the biggest benefit

00:59:25.231 --> 00:59:27.270
that I saw of this one
on firsthand is

00:59:27.271 --> 00:59:28.390
basically when we have

00:59:28.391 --> 00:59:29.820
a new SDN feature that we

00:59:29.821 --> 00:59:31.750
want to implement
in the software.

00:59:31.751 --> 00:59:34.105
Earlier, when we started
off the software

00:59:34.106 --> 00:59:35.460
existed and we figured out how

00:59:35.461 --> 00:59:36.820
to offload the stuff to hardware.

00:59:36.821 --> 00:59:38.810
So, that's the way we
were thinking, but now,

00:59:38.811 --> 00:59:40.860
whenever we are doing
a software feature,

00:59:40.861 --> 00:59:43.280
we say, okay how is hardware
able to support it?

00:59:43.281 --> 00:59:45.050
Because we do have
a hardware limitations

00:59:45.051 --> 00:59:47.125
in terms of tables et cetera.

00:59:47.126 --> 00:59:50.070
So, that is also
another mind shift

00:59:50.071 --> 00:59:53.260
that we see while doing
this entire project.

00:59:53.261 --> 00:59:55.290
So, that's it. Any questions

00:59:55.291 --> 00:59:57.420
or if I don't know
if you have time.

01:00:01.150 --> 01:00:04.230
&gt;&gt; We have a question here.

01:00:05.290 --> 01:00:06.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:00:06.441 --> 01:00:08.395
&gt;&gt; I had a quick question
about migration.

01:00:08.396 --> 01:00:10.110
It seems like there's
a lot of dependency

01:00:10.111 --> 01:00:11.585
on the hardware and whatnot.

01:00:11.586 --> 01:00:12.930
&gt;&gt; So, like live migration?

01:00:12.931 --> 01:00:13.390
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:00:13.391 --> 01:00:14.900
&gt;&gt; Yes, so we do take that

01:00:14.901 --> 01:00:16.850
into account and that
does mean at this point

01:00:16.851 --> 01:00:18.770
of time to upload

01:00:18.771 --> 01:00:20.770
the flows from the hardware
to the software,

01:00:20.771 --> 01:00:22.860
because we need to save the state

01:00:22.861 --> 01:00:25.210
of the VM as it is and
then migrate it out.

01:00:25.211 --> 01:00:26.310
So, we will remove

01:00:26.311 --> 01:00:28.605
the hardware offloads
at that point of time,

01:00:28.606 --> 01:00:30.540
save the state, move
it and then re-enable

01:00:30.541 --> 01:00:33.590
the hardware offloads
at destination.

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:45.610
&gt;&gt; Do you anticipate there to be

01:00:45.611 --> 01:00:48.760
any scalability limits
with FPGAs as

01:00:48.761 --> 01:00:50.570
we scale to even faster and

01:00:50.571 --> 01:00:53.540
host networking say
400 gigabits per second,

01:00:53.541 --> 01:00:55.525
maybe even 800
gigabits per second?

01:00:55.526 --> 01:00:56.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we do see that.

01:00:56.891 --> 01:00:58.050
Actually at this point of time,

01:00:58.051 --> 01:01:00.450
the bottleneck is
the host because we still

01:01:00.451 --> 01:01:03.740
have to do the first packet
processing in the host.

01:01:03.741 --> 01:01:06.090
So, once the offloads

01:01:06.091 --> 01:01:07.830
have been offloaded
to the hardware,

01:01:07.831 --> 01:01:09.550
it is still able to
scale at that level

01:01:09.551 --> 01:01:11.565
but we make continuous
improvements for example,

01:01:11.566 --> 01:01:14.370
you also have a memory constraint

01:01:14.371 --> 01:01:16.030
which is also a bottleneck
in that thing, right?

01:01:16.031 --> 01:01:17.550
Because you have multiple flows.

01:01:17.551 --> 01:01:21.095
You started out with
a single full-sized VM

01:01:21.096 --> 01:01:23.480
and then you go down to
having multiple flows.

01:01:23.481 --> 01:01:25.130
There is a limit
at the end of it,

01:01:25.131 --> 01:01:27.770
and if any packet has to
get process in the host,

01:01:27.771 --> 01:01:29.135
because of hardware limits,

01:01:29.136 --> 01:01:32.355
that is a problem that
we have to look at it.

01:01:32.356 --> 01:01:42.695
&gt;&gt; Yes, I guess
a following question,

01:01:42.696 --> 01:01:45.600
do programmable A6 which is like

01:01:45.601 --> 01:01:49.040
the barefoot switches
have the capability

01:01:49.041 --> 01:01:51.580
to handle all the layers
here and also hit

01:01:51.581 --> 01:01:53.945
the cost point as

01:01:53.946 --> 01:01:56.000
an alternative when
the bandwidth scale up?

01:01:56.001 --> 01:02:02.445
&gt;&gt; Right. Do you have
a question? I thought you said-.

01:02:02.446 --> 01:02:06.480
&gt;&gt; Like the barefoot switches
programmable A6 switches

01:02:06.481 --> 01:02:07.790
have the capabilities to handle

01:02:07.791 --> 01:02:10.435
this as they scale
up and capacity to?

01:02:10.436 --> 01:02:12.040
&gt;&gt; I am not aware but

01:02:12.041 --> 01:02:14.060
one thing is we are doing
all this in the host.

01:02:14.061 --> 01:02:15.470
So, that is something
that we have to

01:02:15.471 --> 01:02:17.010
keep in mind like
we have to support

01:02:17.011 --> 01:02:18.860
the host SDN because that's where

01:02:18.861 --> 01:02:21.755
we try to do all the
policy enforcement.

01:02:21.756 --> 01:02:26.270
&gt;&gt; Done?

01:02:26.271 --> 01:02:29.010
Done. All right. Thank you

